\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Staying Silent, Trump's in Bind On  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pill\b0\par\par\uc2 By Pam Belluck and Sheryl Gay Stolberg \par \uc2 1,783 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 May 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Louisiana wants the Food and Drug Administration to curtail access to the medication. Doing so could cost Republicans at the polls.\par \par \uc2 With the midterm elections less than six months away, Republicans are facing challenging political headwinds, including an unpopular war, escalating inflation and President Trump's sinking approval ratings. Now another issue is putting the administration in a political bind.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Four years after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, which established a national right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1973, it remains widely available, and the number of abortions per year has actually increased slightly. Much of that is due to the availability of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills. \uc2 The Food and Drug Administration\uc2  has so far not walked away from a decision made during the Biden administration to allow access to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill mifepristone through the mail.\par \par \uc2 But a lawsuit against the F.D.A. now threatens that access, and the Trump administration has remained strikingly silent about it, even as the case reached the Supreme Court this month.\par \par \uc2 The suit, brought by the state of Louisiana, led a federal appeals court to temporarily bar a policy allowing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers to prescribe mifepristone through telemedicine and send it by mail. Responding to emergency appeals from two mifepristone manufacturers, the Supreme Court twice paused that ruling and then, last week, restored telemedicine and mail access indefinitely while litigation continues in the lower courts.\par \par \uc2 Through all the back-and-forth, President Trump, who calls himself ''the most pro-life president in history'' stayed mum. His Justice Department, whose job it is to defend the F.D.A. in the case, declined to submit a brief to the Supreme Court, a highly unusual decision.\par \par \uc2 ''That they're the folks who are most directly affected by the litigation and they're not filing anything -- that is shocking,'' said Samuel Bagenstos, who was the general counsel for the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2 , which oversees the F.D.A., during the Biden administration. ''I think it reflects this very difficult political position that the Trump administration is in.''\par \par \uc2 Strategists for both parties say the president is navigating a tricky political calculus. A majority of the public favors widespread access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills. Mr. Trump can stay quiet about the case and incur the wrath of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaders who view his silence as a betrayal and might stay home from the polls in November. Or he can voice his support for restricting access, which would inevitably fire up Democrats and independents to vote in greater numbers. For the moment, at least, he has chosen to remain quiet.\par \par \uc2 But the case is still energizing both sides.\par \par \uc2 ''They don't want this going into the midterm election? Well, unfortunately, they just got it,'' said \uc2 Tony Perkins\uc2 , the president of the \uc2 Family Research Council\uc2 , a conservative group that opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and has been increasingly frustrated by the president's inaction on the issue in his second term. He added: ''It's only going to stay in the news. It's only going to become more of the narrative going into the midterms.''\par \par \uc2 Mini Timmaraju\uc2 , the president and chief executive of \uc2 Reproductive Freedom for All\uc2 , said in a statement that the case underscores the importance of voting this fall.\par \par \uc2 ''Mifepristone is safe, effective and backed by decades of evidence, but anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  extremists dragged this baseless case through the courts as part of their efforts to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide,'' she said, adding: ''This is exactly why the midterm elections are critical. We need leaders at every level of government to stop these attacks.''\par \par \uc2 More than 100 studies and years of F.D.A. reviews have consistently found that mifepristone is safe and that serious complications from taking it are rare. It is the first pill taken in a two-drug medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimen that has become the method used in nearly two-thirds of abortions in the United States. Pills are now mailed to about 100,000 patients per year in states that have banned or severely restricted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The F.D.A. eased restrictions on the strictly regulated drug in 2021, dropping a requirement that patients get mifepristone in person and allowing doctors to prescribe it via telemedicine and send it through the mail.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump's F.D.A. has kept that policy intact, saying it will make a determination after it reviews the drug's safety, a study that administration officials have repeatedly said will not be finished for months. After last week's Supreme Court ruling, the agency promised to ''press forward'' to finish the study and said it would ''provide updates as key milestones are reached.''\par \par \uc2 In fact, that study has not even begun, according to two people with detailed knowledge of the effort. It has been held up for months, these people said, by delays at the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  headquarters, which slowed the F.D.A.'s effort to update a sophisticated set of databases from electronic health records and insurance carriers that are necessary for the research. Opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have accused the agency and the president of slow-walking the study until after the midterms.\par \par \uc2 'A Losing Issue'\par \par \uc2 Opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  animated Republicans for decades, helping to elect candidates and driving voters to the polls. That changed, however, after the court's decision in 2022 to overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Democrats were expected to take a drubbing that year; the party in power generally loses congressional seats in midterm elections. Instead, they suffered relatively minor losses in the House, and they picked up a seat in the Senate. Political strategists in both parties attribute the outcome to the rise of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a salient issue -- a finding confirmed by a post-election analysis by the California Institute of Technology.\par \par \uc2 Whit Ayres, a Republican strategist, said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not been expected to be a major issue this year; voters are consumed with the economy and the war with Iran. Asked why the administration might be reluctant to talk about the mifepristone case, his answer was brief and to the point.\par \par \uc2 ''Because I think they see it as a losing issue,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump was once a hero to the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement. When Mr. Trump first ran for president in 2016, Marjorie Dannenfelser, the president of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America rallied her members to support him after he signed a pledge to appoint Supreme Court justices who would overturn Roe.\par \par \uc2 The support helped propel Mr. Trump to the White House, and when he got there, he made good on that promise. But anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists now fault him because his administration has not used its power to prevent the mailing of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills or the presidential bully pulpit to voice support for a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 ''I think that the administration's been extremely selective with how they have impacted pro-life policy,'' said Gavin Oxley, a spokesman for the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group Americans United for Life, adding that, ''it's notable that the administration has not touched probably the most significant Biden-era \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, and has allowed it to stand.''\par \par \uc2 Last fall, the administration further infuriated anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups, when the F.D.A. granted approval for a new generic version of mifepristone, increasing the number of American manufacturers of the medication to three. Administration officials said at the time that the agency was required by law to approve a generic drug if it was identical to the brand-name drug, as this one was.\par \par \uc2 Sixty-eight percent of American adults oppose banning mifepristone, and 65 percent oppose making it a crime for health care providers to mail \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to patients in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned, according to a December 2025 survey by KFF, a nonpartisan health care research organization.\par \par \uc2 The survey also found that four in 10 Americans were not sure about mifepristone's safety. Ashley Kirzinger, who oversees polling on the issue at KFF, said the organization was conducting additional research on the perception of the drug's safety to learn whether opinion has been shifting as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents criticize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills.\par \par \uc2 Both Ms. Kirzinger and Celinda Lake, a Democratic pollster, said that restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  can swing close elections in Democrats' favor. Ms. Lake said that Democratic women ages 18 to 30 were especially animated by protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 ''It can really mobilize young women,'' she said. ''And that is not trivial.''\par \par \uc2 Louisiana's Aggressive Approach\par \par \uc2 Louisiana, one of 20 states that ban or restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sued in October to block the policy allowing telemedicine and mailing. The state claimed that the policy was adopted without adequate review and that it violated the state's sovereignty by allowing Louisiana residents to receive the medication despite the strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban there.\par \par \uc2 While some Republican strategists would have preferred to see the case remain in the lower courts and out of the headlines, in Louisiana, a red state with a large Catholic population, politicians see little risk in an aggressive approach to shutting down access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  there and across the country. The Louisiana attorney general, Liz Murrill, has been pushing the mifepristone case more aggressively than other conservative states that have filed similar suits, asking the courts to halt mifepristone mailing until the full lawsuit can be adjudicated.\par \par \uc2 Louisiana has also initiated criminal cases against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in New York and California who have been prescribing and sending pills through the mail. And Louisiana's Legislature passed a law to classify the two pills in the typical medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimen, mifepristone and misoprostol, as dangerous controlled substances.\par \par \uc2 Robert Hogan, a political science professor at Louisiana State University, said many Republicans and anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists in the state have made peace with Mr. Trump's relatively quiet approach to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in his second term.\par \par \uc2 ''I think the pro-life people here in the state, who are very much Trump supporters, probably feel that he may be spiking his guns on that issue for political expediency,'' he said. ''And they understand that, in order to keep control of the Congress. But, by heavens, our own people here continue the fight nonetheless.''\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, the state's push has put some Republicans elsewhere in an ill-timed predicament. The White House's posture in the Louisiana lawsuit reflects that, said Katie Keith, director of the Center for Health Policy and the Law at Georgetown University.\par \par \uc2 ''The Trump administration was trying to have it both ways,'' she said, ''and that's how they got themselves into, I think, a bit of a mess here.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260520em5k0003f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
