\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Louisiana Asks Supreme Court to Halt  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Medication Access By Mail\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ann E. Marimow \par \uc2 1,053 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 May 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 19\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The request comes after a federal appeals court temporarily blocked a Food and Drug Administration regulation that has greatly expanded access to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 Louisiana officials on Thursday asked the Supreme Court to let stand, for now, a lower-court ruling that would prevent women nationwide from obtaining a widely used \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication by mail.\par \par \uc2 Last week, the conservative U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit reinstated requirements that pregnant women visit a health care provider in person to obtain the medication, mifepristone. That order caused confusion for providers, patients and pharmacies, as well as companies that manufacture the pill used in nearly two-thirds of abortions nationwide -- which asked the Supreme Court to get involved.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Samuel A. Alito Jr.\uc2 , who oversees emergency requests from the Fifth Circuit, paused the appeals court's order on Monday for one week to give the parties time to respond. The full Supreme Court will review the filings and could decide at any time how to resolve the matter.\par \par \uc2 The Fifth Circuit ruling came after the State of Louisiana sued the Food and Drug Administration to restrict access to mifepristone. State officials said the availability of the medication by mail has allowed abortions to continue in the state despite its near-total ban, which took effect after the Supreme Court eliminated the nationwide right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2022.\par \par \uc2 The F.D.A. first lifted in-person dispensing requirements for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication in 2021 during the Biden administration, and about a quarter of abortions in the United States are now provided through telemedicine.\par \par \uc2 The case has thrust the issue back into the spotlight ahead of the midterm elections and puts the Trump administration in a tricky spot. While the president's Republican base generally opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , restricting access is not considered a broadly winning position in many critical midterm races.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration is conducting a review of the safety of mifepristone. Officials have said in court filings that the review could lead the F.D.A. to change or reaffirm its policies and have asked to delay resolution of this and other related legal disputes until the study is complete.\par \par \uc2 Administration officials declined to comment on the Fifth Circuit ruling, and took the unusual step of submitting no filing to the Supreme Court, even though a government agency, the F.D.A., was the losing party. Justice Alito had asked for responses in the case by 5 p.m. Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Julia Kaye, a lawyer with \uc2 the American Civil Liberties Union\uc2 's Reproductive Freedom Project, said the Trump administration's silence at the Supreme Court is ''incredibly telling, unusual and concerning.''\par \par \uc2 Although the administration did not weigh in by the deadline, Republican members of Congress, including Speaker Mike Johnson and Senator \uc2 John Thune\uc2 , the majority leader, filed a brief siding with Louisiana and asking for the court to allow the pills to be restricted.\par \par \uc2 After the Fifth Circuit ruled in Louisiana's favor on Friday, two manufacturers of mifepristone, \uc2 Danco Laboratories\uc2  and GenBioPro, rushed to the Supreme Court to try to restore nationwide access to the pill by mail.\par \par \uc2 In its filing Thursday, Louisiana said the F.D.A.'s decision to make it easier to obtain mifepristone was based on inadequate or flawed data -- an assertion that medical organizations dispute. Liz Murrill, the state's attorney general, said the regulations had resulted in illegal abortions in the state, and thousands of dollars in Medicaid costs to treat women harmed by mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 Even so, the state said it would agree to a proposal from the manufacturers to keep the Fifth Circuit ruling on hold, provided the Supreme Court agreed to schedule a special argument session to immediately review the matter in depth before the end of its term in late June.\par \par \uc2 ''While this court considers the pending applications, approximately 1,000 abortions are taking place in Louisiana every month, undermining its 'prerogative of protecting unborn life.''' the attorney general said.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration has appeared to try to straddle the issue. In October, the F.D.A. granted approval for a new generic version of mifepristone, increasing the number of American manufacturers of the drug to three.\par \par \uc2 While more than 100 studies have consistently found that mifepristone is safe and that serious complications from taking it are rare, the Trump administration said in September that it was undertaking a new review of its safety.\par \par \uc2 In the Louisiana case, a district court judge, David C. Joseph, in April granted the F.D.A.'s request to pause the litigation and give the agency time to complete its review. Administration officials recently told \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  that the review would probably be finished toward the end of this year, timing that would mean it would not conclude until after the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 On appeal, the Fifth Circuit refused to wait for the review to finish.\par \par \uc2 In its ruling, a three-judge panel echoed the state's arguments that the F.D.A.'s regulations were ''undermining its laws protecting unborn human life'' and ''causing it to spend Medicaid funds on emergency care for women harmed by mifepristone,'' according to the order written by Judge Stuart Kyle Duncan, a Trump appointee. He was joined by Judge Kurt D. Engelhardt, another Trump appointee, and Judge Leslie Southwick, an appointee of President George W. Bush.\par \par \uc2 Mifepristone, the first pill in the two-drug medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimen, was approved by the F.D.A. in 2000. The second, misoprostol, which is also used for other medical conditions, was not affected by the Fifth Circuit ruling.\par \par \uc2 Pharmaceutical companies and former F.D.A. officials have warned the Supreme Court in filings that the Fifth Circuit's order could have broader implications and significantly disrupt the nation's science-based drug-approval system and stifle drug development.\par \par \uc2 Just two years ago, the Supreme Court heard a similar challenge from anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors to the F.D.A.'s loosening of dispensing requirements.\par \par \uc2 Then, the court unanimously held that the doctors challenging the F.D.A.'s regulation of mifepristone had not suffered the sort of direct harm that gave them legal grounds to sue.\par \par \uc2 Pam Belluck contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Pam Belluck contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260508em580004a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
