\par\par \uc2 NONFICTION\par \uc2 Book Review Desk; SECTBR\par \b \uc2 The Body Politic\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jennifer Szalai \par \uc2 1,060 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 March 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 8\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In ''Reproductive Wrongs,'' the classicist Sarah Ruden traces efforts to exert political control over family planning back 2,000 years.\par \par \uc2 REPRODUCTIVE WRONGS: A Short History of Bad Ideas About Women, by Sarah Ruden\par \par \uc2 Sarah Ruden is a translator, literary critic, cultural historian, classical philologist and Quaker. She also happens to be a blistering polemicist on the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a talent she may never have realized if it weren't for the steamrolling of those rights in our current political moment.\par \par \uc2 In ''Reproductive Wrongs: A Short History of Bad Ideas About Women,'' Ruden recalls that she initially wondered if there was something ''rather ridiculous'' in taking on such a live-wire issue. She had been trained in close readings of Homer and Virgil; but the more she looked into historical efforts to exert political control over family planning, the more she realized that her philologist's fascination with language could help her better understand the power of culture and ideology.\par \par \uc2 In her new book, she applies her intellectual firepower to parsing ''communications campaigns'' against reproductive freedoms. Underneath all the moralizing is a relentless push to make intimacy subject to state power: ''Men and their female agents bully women and their partners and entire supportive communities rhetorically, with the language of lofty, authoritative certainty about this most intimate and consequential part of life.''\par \par \uc2 Ruden dates the beginning of ''anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  propaganda'' to the first century B.C., when Ovid wrote two poems that vilify women for ending their pregnancies. In one, the speaker worries that his lover will die from ''the rash ruin of her belly's load''; in the other, the speaker muses that if Venus had ''trespassed on unborn Aeneas,/It would have meant a world bereft of \uc2 Caesars\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Ovid was a ''playboy'' with a complicated love life. Ruden posits that by writing these poems he was trying to ingratiate himself to his patron, the first Roman emperor, Augustus, who promulgated new morality laws penalizing adultery and promoting childbearing. At a time when Rome had just emerged from decades of civil wars, Ovid's poems had the added (and not unrelated) effect of deflecting blame from the men who were responsible: ''It was convenient, as it always is, to lay the fate of a nation distractingly at women's door.''\par \par \uc2 This turns out to be a running theme in ''Reproductive Wrongs'': men making a bloody mess of things and then deciding that the havoc they've wreaked must be a woman's fault. But as much as Augustus wanted to control the private lives of his subjects, he couldn't martial the technologies that such an invasive program would require. ''In the distant past,'' Ruden writes, ''authoritarians' stated ambitions were often in inverse proportion to what they could actually do.'' Only later, in modern contexts, could such totalitarian ambitions be more fully enabled by advances in law enforcement and medicine.\par \par \uc2 ''Reproductive Wrongs'' is an unusual book; it's a brisk and bracing work of cultural history fueled by Ruden's righteous indignation and filigreed with her witty literary criticism. By drawing our special attention to a handful of texts over the last two millenniums, she charts the efflorescence of the right's obsession with sex and sexuality.\par \par \uc2 The Pastoral Epistles of the second century turned Christian motherhood into a ''paralyzing, silencing burden''; Augustine's ''Confessions'' (which Ruden has translated) features some ''joint-popping rhetorical gymnastics.'' Augustine was so tormented by shame over his youthful promiscuity that he clung to absolutist ideas about women, desire and fertility. The child he had with his mistress died as a teenager, and his grief mingled with his guilt. For the rest of his life he fantasized about a ''world without the risks and responsibilities that sex can bring with it.'' Ruden's title for her chapter about ''Augustine's neurosis'' is ''It's the Baby, Stupid.''\par \par \uc2 The other chapter titles are similarly cheeky. ''Hammered'' includes a discussion of a 15th-century book by a Dominican monk titled ''A Hammer Against Evil-Doing Women.'' In ''Hell's Bells,'' Ruden skewers Charles Dickens's mawkish portraits of large families with meager resources. She adds that his ''fertility propaganda'' was politically useful in an age of industrialization that was hungry for cheap labor: ''Victorian -- and, particularly, Dickensian -- ethics were solidly behind the production of factory and mine and cannon fodder.''\par \par \uc2 This uncompromising rhetorical style -- barbed, withering, gently (and sometimes not so gently) mocking -- is bound to turn off some readers. Not to mention that her book is unlikely to convert anyone who doesn't already agree with her positions. Ruden is unwilling to write about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in dulcet tones, unwilling to get caught up in sentimentality or ambivalence, unwilling even to treat an author who identifies as an ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  survivor'' (meaning that her mother had an attempted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but the procedure was botched) as anything more than a pitiable tool in a propaganda campaign.\par \par \uc2 But then Ruden points out that anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists have long understood the power of drawing in an audience and making it feel as if it were ''part of the great drama.'' Messaging in favor of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  was, ''in fatal contrast,'' so incredibly ''dull that I struggle to remember any of it.''\par \par \uc2 At least, that is, until the Dobbs decision of 2022, which effectively overturned Roe v. Wade. Ruden, in her unflagging pursuit of ''great drama,'' may be slightly overstating the dire state of rhetorical complacency pre-Dobbs. But I do think her book reflects a newfound determination on the part of some writers and thinkers to make an energetic case for rights that were long assumed settled but can no longer be taken for granted.\par \par \uc2 Ruden, for her part, believes that anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups have started to use the gauzy language of personal growth -- brimming with sweet platitudes about ''mothers, babies, families, charity, solidarity'' -- to obscure the brute fact that they're in the business not of empowering people but of taking their rights away: ''It is usual for an authoritarian movement to soften its image even as it hardens its heart and moves to entrench and abuse its power.''\par \par \uc2 REPRODUCTIVE WRONGS: A Short History of Bad Ideas About Women | By Sarah Ruden | Liveright | 220 pp. | $27.99\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Szalai is the nonfiction critic at The New York Times.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260322em3m0002x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
