\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Conservative Euphoria Hits Chile as Region Shifts Right\b0\par\par\uc2 By Emma Bubola \par \uc2 1,210 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 March 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The inauguration of Chile's new president, \uc2 Jos\u233\'00\'E9 Antonio Kast\uc2 , is the latest milestone in a broader shift toward conservatism and pro-Trump alignment in the region.\par \par \uc2 Conservative leaders flew across the globe and over the Andes to one of the world's southernmost capitals this week for the inauguration of Chile's right-wing president, \uc2 Jos\u233\'00\'E9 Antonio Kast\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 They arrived in Santiago from Washington, Budapest, Madrid and even farther, for an event that turned a routine transfer of power into a celebration of a movement that is gaining momentum across the hemisphere.\par \par \uc2 ''There is something in the air,'' Kevin Roberts, the president of \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2 , an influential conservative Washington research institute, said in an interview on Tuesday in Santiago, the Chilean capital, where he had also traveled for the inauguration.\par \par \uc2 ''This is an ascendant movement right now,'' he added. ''And it's very exciting.''\par \par \uc2 The inauguration of Mr. Kast, a Catholic and a father of nine who has spent most of his political career railing against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and more recently crime and illegal immigration, follows a string of conservative electoral wins across Latin America in the past year.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kast joins a growing roster of leaders in the hemisphere aligned with President Trump, who has put a greater focus on Latin America and is leaning on his ideological allies to help take on drug cartels and purge Chinese influence from the region.\par \par \uc2 ''What we see today looked like a dream only a few years ago,'' said Jorge Mart\u237\'00\'EDn Fr\u237\'00\'EDas, a Spanish lawmaker in the \uc2 European Parliament\uc2  and a member of Spain's far-right Vox party. He spoke at a conference called ''Freedom and Patriotism,'' which right-wing research groups from across the world had organized at a Santiago hotel.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Frias noted the recent election victories by President \uc2 Javier Milei\uc2  of Argentina, President Nasry Asfura of Honduras and President Santiago Pe\u241\'00\'F1a of Paraguay -- all conservatives.\par \par \uc2 ''There are reasons for happiness, no doubt,'' Mr. Frias added.\par \par \uc2 Still, Latin America's three most populous countries -- Mexico, Colombia and Brazil -- are governed by left-wing leaders, though Colombia and Brazil face highly unpredictable elections this year. In Peru, a right-wing candidate is leading the polls ahead of a general election next month.\par \par \uc2 Experts say the success of right-wing leaders is driven by a surge in anti-incumbent sentiment and growing concerns over crime and security, issues that conservative candidates have successfully capitalized on.\par \par \uc2 While the Trump administration looks on enthusiastically at this trend, it remains unclear whether countries will be willing to work with the United States on security, or move away from China, which has become a dominant trading partner.\par \par \uc2 ''Amid all the celebration among conservatives, there are two big questions,'' said Benjamin N. Gedan, director of the Latin America program at the Stimson Center, a research organization in Washington. ''How long will this last and, in the meantime, will it generate meaningful cooperation?''\par \par \uc2 That skepticism had not been invited to Mr. Kast's inauguration.\par \par \uc2 ''I hope this right-wing wave also reaches Brazil,'' said \uc2 Fl\u225\'00\'E1vio Bolsonaro\uc2 , a son of the former right-wing President of Brazil, \uc2 Jair Bolsonaro\uc2 , who was also in Santiago.\par \par \uc2 Fl\u225\'00\'E1vio Bolsonaro\uc2  plans to challenge Brazil's leftist president, \uc2 Luiz In\u225\'00\'E1cio Lula da Silva\uc2 , in his country's general election in October. Mr. Bolsonaro is taking the place of his father, who is serving a 27-year prison term for plotting a coup to cling to power after losing the last election in 2022.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Lula decided at the last minute not to attend Mr. Kast's inauguration, sending his foreign minister instead. A spokesman for Mr. Lula cited a scheduling conflict, but a person close to the Brazilian presidency said Mr. Kast's invitation to \uc2 Fl\u225\'00\'E1vio Bolsonaro\uc2  played a role in his decision to cancel the visit. The person asked not to be identified because he was speaking about a sensitive issue.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Lula ''declined to come here because he knew that \uc2 Fl\u225\'00\'E1vio Bolsonaro\uc2  would also be present,'' Mr. Bolsonaro said, referring to himself in the third person.\par \par \uc2 Attendees at the inauguration included U.S. Deputy Secretary of State Christopher Landau; the presidents of Argentina, Bolivia and Ecuador; and Mar\u237\'00\'EDa Corina Machado, the leader of Venezuela's main opposition. A large group of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and conservative activists from around the world were also on hand.\par \par \uc2 ''This goes beyond a presidential inauguration,'' said Jes\u250\'00\'FAs Maga\u241\'00\'F1a, a Colombian anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activist. ''It's a change of rhythm in the region.''\par \par \uc2 After discussing the damage inflicted by ''wokism'' and praising Mr. Trump over pisco sours and canap\u233\'00\'E9s at the freedom conference in Santiago, attendees traveled to the coastal Chilean city of Valpara\u237\'00\'EDso on Wednesday for the official inauguration ceremony in the country's Congress.\par \par \uc2 There, Chile's outgoing leftist president, Gabriel Boric, handed off the presidential sash to Mr. Kast.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Boric, a former student leader who was elected on the tails of a social movement demanding economic equality, more rights for women and Indigenous people, left congress in his usual relaxed look, without a tie, driving his own car accompanied by his daughter, whom he has brought to work before, and unmarried partner.\par \par \uc2 Some of his young ministers stood in the crowd as their successors were announced, including Antonia Orellana, a feminist who pushed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation in congress and was replaced as women's minister by an evangelical anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaigner.\par \par \uc2 ''We are going to take back our country,'' Mr. Kast told the crowd on Wednesday night from a balcony of the presidential palace in Santiago, as supporters were handed red ''Make Chile Great Again'' baseball caps sponsored by Passapoga, a local strip club.\par \par \uc2 ''We are going to take back our streets,'' Mr. Kast said. ''We are going to take back our institutions. We are going to take back hope.''\par \par \uc2 There are many questions about how Mr. Kast will govern.\par \par \uc2 He has recently avoided referring to some of his most divisive positions, like his opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . His agenda includes cuts to taxes and public spending, and a crackdown on crime and illegal migration. At his inauguration, he mentioned crime but not \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kast last week attended a summit in Miami, where Mr. Trump welcomed conservative leaders from the region to discuss fighting drug trafficking.\par \par \uc2 He also faces the difficult task of navigating close economic ties with China just as the United States is seeking to roll back China's presence in the region.\par \par \uc2 Chinese state-owned firms control more than half of Chile's electrical grid, and China is Chile's main trading partner and the top buyer of its copper. It is unclear whether Mr. Kast will be willing to curtail China's role in Chile, a challenge that other allies of President Trump across the region, including Mr. Milei of Argentina, also face.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Roberts of the Heritage Foundation was optimistic.\par \par \uc2 ''There's excitement about the United States being in its backyard again,'' he said. ''I anticipate that we will be down here a lot.''\par \par \uc2 Ana Ionova contributed reporting from Rio de Janeiro, Brazil, and John Bartlett from Santiago, Chile.\par \par \uc2 Ana Ionova contributed reporting from Rio de Janeiro, Brazil, and John Bartlett from Santiago, Chile.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260313em3d0004d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
