\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Seeking More Action,  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Foes Gather in D.C.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Elizabeth Dias \par \uc2 1,287 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 January 2026\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 At the March for Life, Vice President \uc2 JD Vance\uc2  acknowledged ''a fear that some of you have that not enough progress has been made.''\par \par \uc2 Last year thousands of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists descended on Washington for their annual March for Life, eagerly hoping that President Trump's second term would accelerate aggressive actions to end remaining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the United States.\par \par \uc2 This year, they arrived on the National Mall with their usual upbeat signs and prayers -- but many were also carrying a gnawing sense of frustration.\par \par \uc2 Even as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents have gained ground and put allies in key policymaking positions, Mr. Trump has at times also infuriated them, exposing fractures among a group whose support has been critical to his success for the past decade.\par \par \uc2 Last fall, the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  approved a generic version of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill mifepristone, which \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents want banned. This month, as Congress debated health care subsidies, Mr. Trump said that Republicans would ''have to be a little flexible'' on the Hyde Amendment, a provision that says federal funds cannot be used for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''This administration has not moved when it absolutely could move,'' Marjorie Dannenfelser, president of Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America, a leading anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group, said in a news conference on Thursday. ''So this is not the direction that we were hoping for.''\par \par \uc2 On Friday, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents showed up hoping to hear something that would revive their spirits. Like at last year's rally, the three most powerful political figures in the country delivered remarks: President Trump via recorded message, and Vice President \uc2 JD Vance\uc2  and Speaker Mike Johnson onstage.\par \par \uc2 Last year, Mr. Vance received enormous cheers as he urged Americans to have more babies, and this week, he announced that he and the second lady, Usha Vance, were expecting their fourth child.\par \par \uc2 At the end of his remarks on Friday, Mr. Vance directly addressed ''the elephant in the room,'' he said, referring to ''a fear that some of you have that not enough progress has been made.''\par \par \uc2 ''I hear you and I understand,'' he said, adding, ''We are going to continue to make strides over the next three years to come.''\par \par \uc2 As important as the politics are, the stakes are existential, he said. ''It is about whether we remain a civilization under God, or whether we will ultimately return to the paganism that has dominated the past,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Activists still champion Mr. Trump as the president who helped them achieve their decades-long goal of ending federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, by filling the Supreme Court in his first term with justices who were willing to overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Now, while Mr. Trump has routinely elevated the place of Christianity in America, he has also consistently prioritized issues like immigration and trade. As the midterm elections approach, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  political leaders are still smarting from how he pushed them to the outskirts of the Republican Party in his 2024 campaign.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, before the march, Trump administration officials invited leading anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists to the White House for a briefing announcing several changes in their favor, including that the \uc2 National Institutes of Health\uc2  would reinstate a ban from Mr. Trump's first term that ended research using human fetal tissue.\par \par \uc2 Officials also told guests that they would be expanding a policy that prevents federal funding from going to international nongovernmental organizations that promote or perform abortions, and that they would investigate \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 's use of $88 million in pandemic-era loans.\par \par \uc2 Leaders of several groups in attendance were pleased with the announcements but also saw them as incremental.\par \par \uc2 Jennie Bradley Lichter, the president of the March for Life, is one of many in the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement who want the Trump administration to restrict access to medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ms. Lichter, a former deputy director of the Domestic Policy Council at the White House during Mr. Trump's first term, noted that effective policymaking could take time, particularly to craft policies that are harder to challenge in court.\par \par \uc2 She believes the Trump administration and her movement are ''on the same page in terms of our general orientation and our first principles, namely that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be limited for a whole host of reasons.''\par \par \uc2 ''We are one year into a four-year term,'' she said. ''We've seen a lot of great pro-life policymaking activity so far.''\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists worry that the Trump administration's actions to limit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are having far-reaching effects.\par \par \uc2 ''Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  extremists are weaponizing every lever of power and level of government to restrict medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the courts, Congress and federal agencies like the F.D.A.,'' said \uc2 Mini Timmaraju\uc2 , the president and chief executive of \uc2 Reproductive Freedom for All\uc2 , an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights group.\par \par \uc2 ''If these efforts succeed, it will devastate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in all 50 states, including those that have already protected and codified \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights,'' she said. ''That's why this November, we must flip the House and Senate and elect governors, attorneys general and lawmakers in the states to enforce the public will.''\par \par \uc2 For some anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists who flew in for the march, the national dynamic is a contrast to advances in their Republican-led states.\par \par \uc2 John Seago, the president of Texas Right to Life, said his home had a ''phenomenal'' year advancing anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. Texas, which already bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in nearly every circumstance, enacted a new law last month that aims to stop doctors in other states from mailing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills into its borders.\par \par \uc2 By contrast, the dynamic in Washington feels ''dire,'' Mr. Seago said. His organization typically focuses on pressuring state lawmakers, but has made it a 2026 goal to focus on federal leaders like Representative Monica De La Cruz, Republican of Texas, who he noted voted to advance the health care subsidies without the Hyde provision.\par \par \uc2 During his 2024 re-election campaign, Mr. Trump's allies pushed a plan to enforce the Comstock Act, a 150-year-old law that Mr. Seago and others hoped would be used to prevent the mailing of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills.\par \par \uc2 So far in his second term, that feels like a reach. Mr. Seago printed 1,500 postcards for March for Life attendees in Washington and Texas to write to the Department of Justice to demand change.\par \par \uc2 ''It is worse than just missed opportunities,'' he said. ''It is actually losing ground.''\par \par \uc2 Speaking with a Domestic Policy Council staff member before the White House meeting on Thursday, Kristan Hawkins, president of Students for Life of America, noted that the administration had invoked 18th-century laws to support its agenda on other issues, like immigration.\par \par \uc2 By comparison, the Comstock Act ''is a fairly modern and new law,'' Ms. Hawkins said. ''What is delaying the progress on that?''\par \par \uc2 The marchers, including many Roman Catholic church groups, received support on Friday from Pope Leo XIV, who sent a written message for the event.\par \par \uc2 ''Please know that you are fulfilling the Lord's command to serve him in the least of our brothers and sisters,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 The note also cited Pope Leo's recent diplomatic message, in which he condemned a ''zeal for war'' that he saw as spreading globally.\par \par \uc2 Garrett Riley, executive director of the Arizona Life Coalition, attended the March for Life again for the first time since 2020, the year Mr. Trump became the first sitting president to speak in person at the event.\par \par \uc2 This time, he's watching the administration closely but focusing his attention on expanding anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pregnancy centers in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 ''Things seem to be, from the outside, stuck or going slow,'' he said. ''We are not going away.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020260124em1o0003r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
