\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 As Clinics Shut,  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Foes Seize Moment\b0\par\par\uc2 By Caroline Kitchener, Ashley Cai and Jamie Kelter Davis \par \uc2 2,406 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 December 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Tamra Call recognized an opportunity for her Christian-based clinic to reach more women than ever before.\par \par \uc2 For months, Ms. Call had been hearing news reports of Planned Parenthoods closing across the country -- forced to shutter amid funding cuts from the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 Now, the closures had reached her hometown, Ames, Iowa. In just a few days, the \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  one block from \uc2 Iowa State University\uc2  would close, leaving thousands of students in Ames with few options for \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care.\par \par \uc2 Obria Medical Clinic -- providing care Ms. Call describes as ''Christ-centered'' and ''life-affirming'' -- was ready to step in.\par \par \uc2 About 50 of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 's nearly 600 clinics have shut down this year, largely because of Republican moves to cut the organization off from government money -- fulfilling a long-held conservative dream to defund the nation's largest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider. Already prohibited from using federal funds for abortions, \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  had relied on government dollars to provide other reproductive services for some two million patients a year, many of whom are low-income and use the clinics as a health care provider of last resort.\par \par \uc2 As Planned Parenthood closures mount, a different kind of organization is seeking to fill the void: anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pregnancy centers. While the typically Christian-based centers have long provided pregnancy tests and counseling for women facing unexpected pregnancies, some have expanded to offer a range of medical services. The founder of the national Obria network, of which the Ames clinic is a member, described Obria as a ''medical brand'' intended to ''attract women out of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 But because the Obria in Ames does not advertise its faith-based approach, patients may struggle to identify the key differences between \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  and the Christian-based clinic. Instead of prescribing birth control, Obria nurses teach patients how to monitor fertility by tracking their menstrual cycles. Instead of performing abortions, they provide counseling they hope will persuade women to carry their pregnancies to term.\par \par \uc2 With \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  numbers continuing to rise three years after the fall of Roe v. Wade, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement sees the \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  closures as an opportunity to stop \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  one community -- and one clinic -- at a time, offering an alternative model for \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care.\par \par \uc2 After hearing that the clinic was preparing to close, Ms. Call walked into the \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  in Ames with a thick stack of Obria fliers.\par \par \uc2 ''I'd love to leave some information for your patients,'' Ms. Call said to the security guard one afternoon in early June. Her fliers pictured women in white coats with stethoscopes, advertising affordable ultrasounds, pap smears and testing for sexually transmitted infections -- all services offered by \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''We just want them to know we are here,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Of the towns and cities where a \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  has closed this year, about half have a pregnancy center that advertises S.T.I. testing, and about 15 percent have one that advertises some form of prenatal or gynecological care, though the offerings are far more limited than those at \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 . Many of the communities also have other kinds of health centers that receive federal funding and offer low-cost \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care.\par \par \uc2 The Obria network -- with 16 locations across five states -- received millions of dollars under the first Trump administration, a grant that ended under President \uc2 Joseph R. Biden Jr.\uc2  The group is deeply invested in the effort to convert pregnancy centers into medical clinics for low-income patients. Unlike most of the approximately 2,700 pregnancy centers nationwide, which are largely unregulated, the Obria clinic in Ames takes insurance and is staffed with licensed nurse practitioners. It is also bound by HIPAA, the federal law protecting sensitive medical information.\par \par \uc2 ''This is the moment for pregnancy centers to announce themselves to the world,'' said John Mize, the chief executive of \uc2 Americans United for Life\uc2 , a national anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group. ''There is an increasing awareness that \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  is struggling, and we have an opportunity to be what they always promised to be, but really never were -- a true social safety net.''\par \par \uc2 Sarah Traxler, an OB-GYN and the chief medical officer of Planned Parenthood North Central States, stressed that anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pregnancy centers ''are not substitutes to \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 ''Any time you are providing medical care based in a religious ideology, you are engaging in coercion and paternalism,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 As soon as Ms. Call left the \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  building, the clinic manager dumped her fliers in the recycling bin.\par \par \uc2 ''They saw the news and jumped to, 'Well, we can just take these patients,''' said Lacee Be, who ran daily operations at the clinic before it closed in mid-June. ''It doesn't feel ethical.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Call did not see it that way. As a Christian, she said, she felt called to serve vulnerable women who might consider \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and ''let the oppressed go free,'' one of her favorite Bible passages. To Ms. Call, \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  pushed its patients toward a risky, destructive lifestyle that could eventually leave them broken.\par \par \uc2 This was her moment to show them another way, she said.\par \par \uc2 In the months that followed, Obria added a second nurse practitioner, revamped its social media with a new communications coordinator, and started an advertising campaign on the bus system students use to get around Iowa State. Ms. Call and her staff wrote to donors about the closure, asking them to ''join us in praying Obria becomes a key provider to fill gaps left by \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 's exit.''\par \par \uc2 Since the \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  closed, records show, the number of patients seeking care at Obria has nearly doubled.\par \par \uc2 A Campus Health Care Hub\par \par \uc2 At Iowa State, students knew where to find \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 . The one-story brick building was a three-minute walk from campus, tucked between a row of student apartments and a country-rock bar known for its karaoke and crowded dance floor.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  intentionally integrated itself into campus life, Ms. Be said, with a reputation for confidential services that wouldn't show up on a parent's insurance statement. Whenever a student dropped by for a morning-after pill, Ms. Be handed it over, no charge.\par \par \uc2 ''This is free for any student at Iowa State,'' she would say. ''Tell your friends.''\par \par \uc2 Abortions made up about 40 percent of the services offered at the Ames clinic, according to \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , with Iowa law banning the procedure after six weeks of pregnancy. Some patients received gender transition treatment. Most came for contraception and S.T.I. testing.\par \par \uc2 ''I didn't have a primary care doctor when I was a freshman,'' said Jasmine Lambert, a former Iowa State student who stayed in Ames and has gone to \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  for contraception for seven years. She knew \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  was not ''going to be weird about providing birth control,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Now, Ms. Lambert drives 40 minutes to get her birth control from the only primary care doctor she could find with available appointments.\par \par \uc2 The former \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  office was locked during a recent visit, with blackened windows, the lawn littered with crushed beer cans and a Joker mask from Halloween.\par \par \uc2 ''I feel a loss of sense of security,'' Ms. Lambert said.\par \par \uc2 The Ames clinic is one of four Planned Parenthoods to close in Iowa this year, leaving two open locations, in Iowa City and Des Moines. Another 11 clinics have closed in the neighboring states of Minnesota, Illinois and Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 Many \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  clinics had already been struggling before President Trump took office. A \uc2 New York Times\uc2  investigation earlier this year revealed a network that operated on a shoestring budget with aging equipment and extremely low salaries -- sometimes failing to provide quality care.\par \par \uc2 In Iowa, state lawmakers have targeted \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 's finances for years, said Ruth Richardson, the president and chief executive of Planned Parenthood North Central States. But recent federal cuts were the death blow, she added.\par \par \uc2 This spring, the Trump administration withheld a nearly $3 million grant allotted to her clinics through a federal family planning initiative. Then came news of Mr. Trump's sweeping policy bill, which was devised to prevent \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  affiliates that provide abortions from accepting payments from Medicaid, the government insurance program for low-income Americans.\par \par \uc2 According to \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , that defunding provision puts approximately one-third of its locations at risk of closure.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  officials stressed that their patients still had options: Those in Ames could drive 40 miles to the branch in Des Moines or talk to a provider through telehealth.\par \par \uc2 But when one sophomore at Iowa State noticed an unfamiliar smell in her underwear a few months ago, she wanted to talk to a medical professional in person, somewhere nearby.\par \par \uc2 The student health clinic was not an option, the sophomore said, because she worried her parents might ask about medications that showed up on her student bill. If she had an S.T.I., she said, she would never want them to know.\par \par \uc2 The sophomore, who spoke on the condition of anonymity to discuss a private health condition, searched online for clinics that treated bacterial vaginosis and sexually transmitted infections.\par \par \uc2 A friend of hers had gone to \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , she said -- ''but it wasn't around anymore.''\par \par \uc2 She went to Obria instead.\par \par \uc2 'We Are Ready to Receive These Women'\par \par \uc2 Before Obria launched its fall advertising campaign, the clinic's new communications coordinator researched the question that would shape her approach: ''Why do women choose \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 ?''\par \par \uc2 The same adjectives popped up again and again, said the coordinator, Lauren Britton, who was hired into the new role several weeks after the \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  closed. \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  patients wanted \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care that was ''affordable'' and ''comprehensive,'' she found.\par \par \uc2 Those adjectives described Obria, too, Ms. Britton said. And so those were the words Obria leaders would splash across the sides of the campus buses -- their best opportunity to reach the women of Iowa State.\par \par \uc2 ''We want people to know: \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  has closed, but there is still \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care in Ames,'' Ms. Call said. ''We are ready to receive these women.''\par \par \uc2 A 10-minute drive from campus, Obria Medical Clinic looks like a doctor's office, with hard-backed black chairs and houseplants in the lobby. A framed document above the water cooler certifies that the clinic complies with HIPAA, which governs medical facilities, but few pregnancy centers.\par \par \uc2 The only hint of Obria's Christian orientation comes from the reading materials selected for the waiting room: Instead of People or Vogue, patients can leaf through HomeLife, a magazine published by the \uc2 Southern Baptist Convention\uc2 , or a Bible.\par \par \uc2 Most patients do not know that Obria is a Christian clinic, Ms. Call said.\par \par \uc2 ''I think there's just a lot of baggage, for lack of a better word, that can come with an overt message that we are a Christian-based organization,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 At their appointments, some patients are also surprised to learn that Obria does not provide birth control -- a position the clinic takes to promote what the staff call a more ''sacred view of sex.''\par \par \uc2 Pregnancy centers began opening in the late 1960s and 1970s as small Christian mom-and-pop shops, often run by volunteers. The movement to provide ultrasounds and eventually other medical services started in the early 2010s, said Andrea Swartzendruber, an associate professor at the University of Georgia who studies anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pregnancy centers.\par \par \uc2 Unlike \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , pregnancy centers very rarely have doctors on staff. Medical appointments at the Obria in Ames are handled by two full-time licensed nurse practitioners, with some oversight from a retired OB-GYN who volunteers with the clinic, reviewing charts remotely and occasionally coming in for consultations. The providers, who said they refer patients out to other local clinics or hospitals for complex cases, undergo an extensive credentialing process to take Medicaid and insurance, rather than offering all services for free, as most pregnancy centers do.\par \par \uc2 While insurance payments yield just 6 percent of the Ames clinic's $720,000 annual budget, Ms. Call said, Obria considers the process worth the hassle.\par \par \uc2 ''It adds legitimacy to us as a medical clinic,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 The clinic gets the rest of its money from private donors and grants, Ms. Call said. That includes nearly $500,000 over three years from the state of Iowa -- which, like other Republican-led states, has directed considerable funding to pregnancy centers since Roe fell.\par \par \uc2 Many medical professionals remain skeptical of Obria, including one former Obria chief executive who said last year in an interview with Elle that the network was ''misrepresenting'' itself as a group of medical clinics.\par \par \uc2 ''The movement tried to come up with something to make themselves more legitimate, other than 'We want to save babies from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,' and found a way to get federal monies,'' said Dawn Hughes, who led the group for two years and did not respond to a request for comment. She left after unsuccessfully pushing for the Obria board to start providing contraception, according to two people with knowledge of the events.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Call reiterated that Obria locations are fully licensed medical clinics, and stressed the clinic's commitment to transparency. The Obria in Ames does not advertise \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related services on its website, a difference from many centers in its cohort that do so to attract women who want to end their pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 ''We have always wanted to be above board and not try to manipulate people in any way,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 When the Iowa State sophomore went to Obria this fall, she was nervous to talk openly about her symptoms, she said during an interview at the clinic shortly after her appointment. ''But just being in here and talking was a weight off my back.''\par \par \uc2 The staff spent time getting to know her, she said. She told them about her Christian parents back home. How she didn't want them to know she was having sex.\par \par \uc2 The 19-year-old had no idea she had gone to a Christian-based clinic.\par \par \uc2 Since her appointment, she said, several friends at school have asked her where to go for their own S.T.I. tests.\par \par \uc2 She told them about Obria.\par \par \uc2 David A. Fahrenthold and Katie Benner contributed reporting. Kitty Bennett contributed research.\par \par \uc2 David A. Fahrenthold and Katie Benner contributed reporting. Kitty Bennett contributed research.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251223elcn0004b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
