\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Despite Red-State Efforts,  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rate Nationwide Still Climbs\b0\par\par\uc2 By Soumya Karlamangla \par \uc2 1,153 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 December 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The state divide over abortions has only deepened since the Supreme Court decision. But research shows the number of abortions has climbed.\par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, the North Dakota Supreme Court reinstated a near-total ban on abortions. Texas enacted a law that lets private citizens sue out-of-state doctors who mail \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medications into the state.\par \par \uc2 And California approved legislation to protect those very same doctors, allowing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to be sent into Texas and other states without the name of the patient or prescriber on the prescription label.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  in 2022 overturned the landmark \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision Roe v. Wade, granting state governments far more control over regulating abortions than they had had for nearly 50 years.\par \par \uc2 In the three years since, the divide between states that allow abortions and those that severely limit or ban them has only continued to deepen. In some cases, states are battling each other as they try to enforce their restrictions or protect access across state lines.\par \par \uc2 ''The U.S. is becoming a tale of two countries in terms of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy,'' said Dr. Ushma Upadhyay, a professor and a public health scientist at the \uc2 University of California, San Francisco\uc2 . But, she added, ''All of this legislation will never take away from the fact that women will continue to need \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, and continue to get \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care.''\par \par \uc2 Despite red states passing a spate of laws restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in recent years, research shows that the number of abortions nationwide has climbed every year since 2022, when the Supreme Court's decision in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization ended a woman's constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That's largely because doctors in states that permit abortions are providing more abortions to women living in states where the procedure is banned or highly restricted.\par \par \uc2 Since 2022, the proportion of patients who travel out of state for abortions has increased substantially, studies have found. Carbondale, a small city in the southern tip of Illinois, illustrates this trend: three clinics there, within driving distance from 10 states that ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , provided 11,000 abortions last year, nearly all to out-of-state residents.\par \par \uc2 And while more patients are traveling out of state for the procedure, experts say it is telehealth, in which doctors mail \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medications so that pregnancies can be ended at home, that is driving much of the increase.\par \par \uc2 Last year, one out of four abortions nationwide was provided via telehealth, compared to 5 percent before 2022, according to WeCount, a study led by the Society of Family Planning. And in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was totally banned, 99 percent of abortions were provided by telehealth.\par \par \uc2 ''The reality is people are getting abortions, people are providing abortions, and the post-Dobbs environment is not stopping them,'' said David Cohen, a law professor at \uc2 Drexel University\uc2  who co-wrote the book ''After Dobbs.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cohen pointed out that the recent North Dakota court decision to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would likely have little impact on the ground because the state's climate had been so hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for years. There aren't any providers left in the state to push out.\par \par \uc2 And in Missouri, where anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates are trying to roll back access with a ballot measure, women have already become accustomed to leaving the state or getting telehealth abortions because the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  landscape has fluctuated so much in recent years, experts say.\par \par \uc2 ''Individual state borders no longer had the same meaning they once had,'' said Carole Joffe, a professor at the \uc2 University of California, San Francisco\uc2 , who co-authored the book with Mr. Cohen. ''What we can conclude from the post-Dobbs era is that the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -providing community and women themselves have developed very creative modes of resistance.''\par \par \uc2 The popularity of telehealth, particularly in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned, has made it the latest frontier for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation in both red and blue states.\par \par \uc2 Last week, Texas enacted a law that aims to stop doctors from other states from mailing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to Texas.\par \par \uc2 Texas, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned in almost all circumstances, is one of 20 states that now ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or restrict the procedure earlier in pregnancy than the standard set by Roe v. Wade. But roughly 35,800 people got abortions in Texas last year, virtually all through a telehealth provider, according to the WeCount data.\par \par \uc2 The new Texas law, known as HB 7, allows private citizens to file lawsuits for at least $100,000 against anyone who prescribes or mails \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication to Texas residents. It is the most ambitious effort yet to halt abortions through the telehealth process.\par \par \uc2 ''What anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lawmakers and advocates are trying to do is disrupt the new realities of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care in 2025,'' said Kelly Baden, vice president for public policy at the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a research organization that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. ''This was never just about overturning Roe v. Wade -- this is about really trying to stop all abortions anywhere, any way they can.''\par \par \uc2 Those who oppose telehealth abortions say that doctors have overstepped by providing abortions for women who live in states where the procedure is not allowed. Texas Right to Life, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group, heralded the law for targeting ''the underground \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  industry,'' led by activists ''hiding in liberal states.''\par \par \uc2 So far, medical providers have said the law won't stop them from providing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication to people in Texas.\par \par \uc2 Blue states this year have strengthened their shield laws, which protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers from out-of-state prosecutions and are intended to allow them to keep prescribing medicines for people out-of-state. In at least eight states, including California and Massachusetts, officials are prevented from obeying subpoenas, extradition requests and other legal actions that states with bans take against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers -- a stark departure from typical interstate practices of cooperating in legal matters.\par \par \uc2 This summer, Republican attorneys general wrote to congressional leaders saying they were ''asking Congress to assess the constitutional authority it may have to pre-empt shield laws.'' The letter described the laws as ''blatant attempts to interfere with states' ability to enforce criminal laws within their borders and disrupt our constitutional structure.''\par \par \uc2 Instead, Democratic lawmakers in states including New York, Washington and Colorado this year added safeguards that allow doctors to remove their full names from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication prescriptions for privacy.\par \par \uc2 California went even farther. In Colorado, for example, providers still must put the practice's name, if not the individual doctor's, on the prescription label. But California's new law allows providers to dispense \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drugs without including the name of the doctor, pharmacist or patient on the label.\par \par \uc2 ''California stands for a woman's right to choose,'' Gov. \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  said in a statement when he signed the law in September.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251210elca0004j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
