\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Justices Seem Supportive of N.J. Anti- \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Center\b0\par\par\uc2 By Abbie VanSickle \par \uc2 875 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 December 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A majority of the justices appeared supportive of arguments by a crisis pregnancy center that it should be able to challenge a subpoena from the state's attorney general in federal court.\par \par \uc2 A majority of the \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  on Tuesday appeared to believe an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pregnancy center should be able to challenge a subpoena demanding its donor information in federal court.\par \par \uc2 The dispute focused on a subpoena issued by the New Jersey Office of the Attorney General in November 2023, seeking information, including donor names and contact information, from First Choice Women's Resource Centers, a group of five centers that seek to dissuade women from having abortions.\par \par \uc2 When the organization received the subpoena, it attempted to challenge the request in federal court, claiming it violated the group's First Amendment freedoms of speech and association. Federal courts dismissed the case, finding that the lawsuit was premature because the subpoena had not yet been ordered to go into effect by a state court judge.\par \par \uc2 A federal appeals court upheld that ruling, so First Choice asked the \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  to weigh in.\par \par \uc2 Although the case involved the narrow procedural question of whether First Choice had properly brought its lawsuit in federal court, the backdrop of the dispute loomed large.\par \par \uc2 New Jersey's attorney general, Matthew J. Platkin, issued the subpoena in the year following the \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2 's decision overturning Roe v. Wade. That ruling, Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, led states throughout the country to re-examine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and put a spotlight on centers like First Choice.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates have argued that such organizations mislead women seeking abortions. The offices often look similar to medical facilities for pregnant women, featuring signs offering ultrasounds and literature describing pregnancy tests, but the advocates say they actually exist to dissuade women from ending their pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 If the clinics win, it could clear the way for them to continue their First Amendment challenge in the federal courts. If they lose, it could limit their ability to challenge the subpoena, at least until further state court action in New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 During the argument, the lawyer for First Choice, Erin Morrow Hawley, asserted that First Choice had been harmed ''the moment it received'' the subpoena and argued that the state's action had a chilling effect on the organization's donors.\par \par \uc2 ''First Choice is simply asking for its day in federal court here,'' said Ms. Hawley, a lawyer for \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2 , a conservative Christian advocacy group that has litigated a number of cases before the \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2 , including a 2023 case in which the group unsuccessfully challenged federal approval of a widely available \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill. Ms. Hawley, a former law clerk to Chief Justice \uc2 John G. Roberts Jr.\uc2 , is married to Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2 , Republican of Missouri, who was in the audience for the argument.\par \par \uc2 A lawyer for the New Jersey attorney general argued that the subpoena had not had a chilling effect on the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group's donors, but Chief Justice Roberts appeared skeptical, noting the breadth of the information sought by the attorney general.\par \par \uc2 ''So you don't think it might have an effect on future potential donors to the organization to know that their name, phone number, address, et cetera, could be disclosed as a result of the subpoena?" the chief justice asked.\par \par \uc2 When the lawyer, Sundeep Iyer, noted that the state issues subpoenas to all sorts of organizations to ferret out evidence of misleading consumer information, such as, potentially, from car dealerships, the chief justice quickly responded: ''Your friends on the other side don't represent a car dealership.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Iyer asserted that the state's subpoena was not focused on investigating anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  donors. Rather, he said, state officials were concerned that First Choice's website may have misled donors, tricking them into thinking they were giving to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider, rather than to an organization opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''We have no interest in seeking enforcement against any of these donors,'' Mr. Iyer said.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  pointedly asked Mr. Iyer whether the state had received any complaints from consumers about misleading information on the group's website.\par \par \uc2 When Mr. Iyer replied that the state had received no complaints, Justice Thomas responded that a subpoena seemed a ''burdensome'' way of deciphering whether an organization ''has a confusing website.''\par \par \uc2 One of the court's liberals, Justice \uc2 Elena Kagan\uc2 , also appeared skeptical, saying that ''an ordinary person'' would not find it ''reassuring'' to be told they need not be concerned about a government subpoena since it required court approval.\par \par \uc2 The case has led to some unusual alliances. Both the Trump administration and \uc2 the American Civil Liberties Union\uc2  -- often fierce opponents on a range of issues from immigration to transgender rights -- weighed in to support the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group.\par \par \uc2 The A.C.L.U. had filed a brief arguing that such subpoenas for donor lists could have a chilling effect on free speech rights. The civil liberties group asserted that state attorneys general across the political spectrum had employed similar tactics, citing subpoenas issued by the Florida attorney general related to restaurants that had hosted drag shows.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251203elc30003p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
