\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 States Vow To Make Up Clinic Funds Cut by U.S.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Grace Ashford \par \uc2 896 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 October 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 21\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 President Trump's signature policy bill has barred clinics that provide abortions from receiving any Medicaid payments, putting many at risk of closure.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic governors of New York and California announced on Thursday that they would step in to backfill tens of millions of dollars in lost federal funding to \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  after the Trump administration barred clinics that provide abortions from receiving Medicaid payments.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  of New York pledged to fully cover one year of missing Medicaid funds for \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  in the state, roughly $35 million. In California, Gov. \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  set aside $140 million, which would ensure funding for the organization until the Legislature is back in session next year.\par \par \uc2 In July, President Trump signed into law his signature policy bill, which included a one-year ban on state Medicaid payments to any health care nonprofit that offers abortions and that received more than $800,000 in federal funding in 2023.\par \par \uc2 Since the law's passage, \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  clinics have been forced to choose between providing abortions and staying open. Roughly 200 clinics across the country -- one-third of all \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  locations -- are at risk of closure, underscoring the real possibility of a country where reproductive care is inaccessible for a vast swath of American women.\par \par \uc2 A spokeswoman for \uc2 Planned Parenthood Federation of America\uc2 , Angela Vasquez-Giroux, said in a statement that ''this was always the intent'' of the law -- to ''shut down \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  health centers, strip access to essential health care from patients, and make it harder for everyone, everywhere to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- even in states where it's legal.''\par \par \uc2 The investments represent some of the most muscular efforts yet by states to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access from the Trump administration's cuts. New York and California are the largest states yet to commit to filling the funding gap, following the lead of Washington, Hawaii, New Mexico, Colorado, Massachusetts and New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 Both Mr. Newsom and Ms. Hochul described the commitment of funding as a statement of their states' values.\par \par \uc2 ''California is a reproductive freedom state, and this latest investment continues to show our belief in protecting access to essential health care in times of distress,'' Mr. Newsom said in a statement on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 In an interview, Ms. Hochul noted that the country's first women's rights convention took place in New York State, in Seneca Falls. ''This is a continuation of, in my mind, the march for women's rights started back in 1848,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 In New York, \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  operates through its five local affiliates, which run 47 clinics. More than 60 percent of those are in rural areas, where there are few other health care options. The clinics offer prenatal care and cancer screenings in addition to reproductive services.\par \par \uc2 Since Roe v. Wade was overturned in 2022, Ms. Hochul, who is seeking re-election next year, has promised to make New York a safe haven for women in need of abortions. Among other moves, she has set aside tens of millions of dollars for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and signed legislation to shield New York physicians who prescribe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication to out-of-state patients from prosecution by other states.\par \par \uc2 In November, New York voted to amend the state Constitution to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  and independent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in New York nonetheless rely on Medicaid for funding. Last year, \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  clinics in New York received $35 million in Medicaid payments; according to the organization, roughly half its patients are on Medicaid.\par \par \uc2 Providers are already prohibited by law from using federal money to pay for abortions, but they have long used it to cover other costs. In August, \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  confirmed it would allow its local affiliates to decide whether to stop providing abortions so as to continue collecting federal money to fund other services, like birth control and testing for sexually transmitted infections.\par \par \uc2 Thus far, \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  in Wisconsin has agreed to stop providing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services.\par \par \uc2 The provision to withhold Medicaid funds is the latest step in a decades-long campaign by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents to defund \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 . Even before the passage of the new law in July, Arkansas, Missouri and Texas had moved to bar \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  clinics from receiving Medicaid payments.\par \par \uc2 The coordinated attacks have left the clinics struggling, despite their name recognition and considerable public support. In February, \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  reported that the organization was facing complaints of poor care and failed procedures, and that some affiliates had begun to scale back services.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  in New York announced it would no longer perform abortions after 20 weeks of pregnancy. State law allows abortions through 24 weeks, but the organization said it could no longer afford the ''deep sedation'' required for later procedures.\par \par \uc2 Donations to \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 's national organization go toward litigation and lobbying to expand \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access nationwide, rather than toward health care services. The 47 regional affiliates operate independently and must seek donations on their own.\par \par \uc2 In the interview, Ms. Hochul said that Republican members of Congress who supported the law had failed their constituents.\par \par \uc2 ''In the meantime,'' she said, ''I'm not going to stand here and let women across our state suffer.''\par \par \uc2 Katie Benner contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Katie Benner contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251025elap0003o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
