\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Kirk's Debate Style: Provoke, Engage, Twist Facts, Repeat.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ken Bensinger and Charlie Smart \par \uc2 3,364 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 October 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 18\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Charlie Kirk may be best remembered for arguing in public.\par \par \uc2 A cornerstone of Mr. Kirk's devoted conservative following was his twice-yearly tours of universities around the country. For hours, he would cheerfully perch on a folding chair and challenge students and the public to, as he called it, ''Prove Me Wrong.''\par \par \uc2 By tackling hot-button issues like \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and trans rights, Mr. Kirk created content that became perfect fodder for brand-building on social media. Curated clips highlighting his wins, promoted with captions describing him as ''destroying'' liberals, have racked up tens of millions of views on TikTok, \uc2 YouTube\uc2  and \uc2 Instagram\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Since his assassination last month, Mr. Kirk has been lionized, mostly by those on the right but also by some who did not share his views, as a champion of free speech and an interrogator of viewpoints that spanned the political spectrum.\par \par \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  reviewed more than four dozen of Mr. Kirk's debates, stretching back to 2017, and discussed them with four debate coaches and university professors.\par \par \uc2 The Times review -- which examined content, tone, techniques and other hallmarks of each confrontation -- reveals how Mr. Kirk used the debate format to deliver a consistent hard-line message while orchestrating highly shareable moments.\par \par \uc2 This genre of debate, which Mr. Kirk helped pioneer, is now a template that other social media personalities across the political spectrum have increasingly adopted. Here's a look at how Mr. Kirk constructed his viral confrontations.\par \par \uc2 April 24, 2025\par \par \uc2 University of Wyoming\par \par \uc2 Hyperbole and Go-To Quips\par \par \uc2 One of Mr. Kirk's favorite topics -- and the title of one of his books -- was ''The College Scam,'' which reflected his opinion that college was a waste of time and money. (Mr. Kirk himself dropped out of community college.)\par \par \uc2 Although he elsewhere had stated that the point of college is ''to become a well-rounded citizen of what is good, true and beautiful,'' at this event he suggested the primary goal of higher education was to compete with China and that majors like women's studies were ''irrelevant.''\par \par \uc2 This line of argument was most likely to provoke emotional reactions from opponents -- who after all were college students invested in their own education. And rhetoric experts agree that getting upset or defensive is a surefire way to end up on the losing side of a debate.\par \par \uc2 Kirk: Does college provide skills to the majority of people that go? Does a woman's study degree give you any skills that is going to help us fight China?\par \par \uc2 Student: Well, you're taking, and I know why you're doing this. You're --\par \par \uc2 Kirk: That's a majority of degrees and people that go to college are in the social sciences, are in communications, and they're in irrelevant degrees. A majority. So a majority of the 21 million people that are in college right now are there studying stuff that you and I would laugh at.\par \par \uc2 Student: Oh, I wouldn't laugh at that at all. I think that people go to college for a variety of reasons.\par \par \uc2 Part of Mr. Kirk's advantage came from repetition. He debated hundreds of people and learned how to shape conversation -- and where to drop in canned audience pleasers. This wisecrack about ''North African lesbian poetry,'' delivered at the \uc2 University of Wyoming\uc2  in April, was hardly spontaneous.\par \par \uc2 Kirk: No, I know that, but do you think that when we're looking at American competitiveness, studying North African lesbian poetry is going to make us more competitive?\par \par \uc2 The Times found at least four other examples of him using the exact same line in the past two years.\par \par \uc2 Cal State, Fullerton | April 14, 2022 ''If you ask me, we have way more than enough people that studied North African lesbian poetry.''\par \par \uc2 ''The Charlie Kirk Show'' | July 31, 2024 ''Because they're willing to send their kid to go study North African lesbian poetry at \uc2 Arizona State University\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Gavin Newsom interview | March 6, 2025 ''You go to Cal to go study North African lesbian poetry? Like ... ''\par \par \uc2 Cambridge Union | May 19, 2025 ''We have a lot of people going to university to go study North African lesbian poetry.''\par \par \uc2 Oct. 15, 2024\par \par \uc2 Northern Arizona University\par \par \uc2 Engaging and Subduing\par \par \uc2 Even on the most progressive campuses, Mr. Kirk's debates drew large and boisterous contingents of supporters, cheering him on in MAGA hats. But while he traded barbs with his adversaries, he often discouraged heckling. By making space for opposing viewpoints, he enhanced his image as a defender of free speech while centering his own argument.\par \par \uc2 Additionally, by restraining his audience from shouting down his opponents, Mr. Kirk insulated himself from seeming like a bully to many viewers of clips shared on social media.\par \par \uc2 Opponent: I'll make you guys wait on this.\par \par \uc2 Crowd: Zero knowledge!\par \par \uc2 Opponent: Ooh! Zero knowledge?\par \par \uc2 Kirk: Guys, please don't heckle, just I'm, I want to try to understand because these words have a lot of meaning.\par \par \uc2 Opponent: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 Kirk: But you can't get -- I know it's hard on the spot. I just want you to think about it because if you say Trump is a racist --\par \par \uc2 Opponent: Yup.\par \par \uc2 Kirk: -- You should be able to easily give a definition of what that word means.\par \par \uc2 March 13, 2025\par \par \uc2 University of Tennessee\par \par \uc2 Playing to the Crowd\par \par \uc2 During this debate at the \uc2 University of Tennessee\uc2  in March, a student asked Mr. Kirk whether he thought abortions should be allowed when a mother's life is in danger.\par \par \uc2 Student: What happens if you need a medical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? If the mother will die if she does not get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , what's your stance on that?\par \par \uc2 Kirk: So the only place where medical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is necessary is before 20 weeks, which is incredibly rare.\par \par \uc2 Student: What if she will die? What if the mother will die?\par \par \uc2 Kirk: Hold on a second.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kirk, who opposed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , responded by proposing a cesarean section as a better alternative, and then asked the student if she knew what the procedure was. It was a tactic Mr. Kirk frequently used: asking opponents to define a term, so he could score easy points by making them appear uninformed if they could not.\par \par \uc2 Kirk: So let's take, they -- for example -- this is a very important question. Let's say it's 27 weeks. OK? And the mother will die. Why shouldn't, you could agree, why don't we just have the baby delivered by cesarean section? You know what a cesarean section is, right?\par \par \uc2 Student: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Kirk: What is a cesarean section?\par \par \uc2 Student: A C-section?\par \par \uc2 Kirk: Yeah, why wouldn't we just put the baby out by C-section instead of terminating it?\par \par \uc2 In this case, the student knew what a C-section was, so Mr. Kirk quickly pivoted to another common strategy by addressing allies in the crowd -- ''this is for you guys'' -- with advice on how they should respond to the question if it ever came up in their own lives.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kirk's claim that C-sections are safer than abortions is widely disputed by medical professionals. The rate of medical complications during C-sections is more than four times that of abortions, according to research published in the American Journal of Obstetrics and Gynecology and Obstetrics & Gynecology.\par \par \uc2 Student: Because if the mother will die --\par \par \uc2 Kirk: No, no --\par \par \uc2 Student: -- it's not safe to do that.\par \par \uc2 Kirk: But you remove the baby. Threat removed.\par \par \uc2 Student: I understand. I know what a C-section is.\par \par \uc2 Kirk: But it's actually safer than an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . So every time they say -- this is for you guys -- they say ''Oh, you need to have \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for medical reasons,'' respond: ''Then have a C-section.''\par \par \uc2 Later in this debate with the same student at the \uc2 University of Tennessee\uc2 , Mr. Kirk shifted to more extreme rhetoric, calling \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ''worse'' than the Holocaust.\par \par \uc2 Ben Voth, a professor of rhetoric and director of debate and speech at \uc2 Southern Methodist University\uc2 , cited ''discussing forbidden topics and upholding forbidden arguments'' as a muscular and emotionally resonant strategy to which Mr. Kirk regularly returned.\par \par \uc2 Kirk: It is never right to justify the mass elimination or termination of people under the guise of saying they're unwanted. That's how we get Auschwitz. That is how we get the greatest horrors of the 20th century. Those people are unwanted.\par \par \uc2 Student: So you're comparing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the Holocaust?\par \par \uc2 Kirk: Absolutely, I am. In fact, it's worse. It's worse!\par \par \uc2 Student: It's really not. I don't think it is.\par \par \uc2 Kirk: It's 45 million babies. It's nearly eight times worse than the Holocaust. What's the moral difference between a small baby in the womb and a grown Jew who was killed at Auschwitz? What's the moral difference? One is older?\par \par \uc2 Student: One of them is a person.\par \par \uc2 (Mr. Kirk also mentioned ''45 million babies,'' presumably a reference to a number of abortions, without specifying where or over what period of time they took place.)\par \par \uc2 After more back-and-forth, Mr. Kirk laid a rhetorical trap. When the student replied ''human'' to his question about what species an embryo is, he claimed victory, saying that implied embryos deserve human rights.\par \par \uc2 Aaron Kall, who coached the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2  to its first national championship as director of debate last year, called that kind of move a ''turn'' -- using the other person's own words to prove your point. ''It has more impact when you turn your opponent's argument to your advantage,'' Mr. Kall said.\par \par \uc2 Kirk: What species is the baby in the womb?\par \par \uc2 Student: It's a human, but it's an embryo.\par \par \uc2 Kirk: Therefore they should have human rights!\par \par \uc2 Student: No!\par \par \uc2 Kirk: They should have human rights!\par \par \uc2 Student: I do not agree.\par \par \uc2 Kirk: So small humans, no human rights; grown humans, human rights.\par \par \uc2 Student: It's not a small human. It's an embryo.\par \par \uc2 Kirk: You just said it was a human species!\par \par \uc2 Student: It's not a living human child.\par \par \uc2 March 14, 2024\par \par \uc2 California State University, Fullerton\par \par \uc2 Citing Statistics, True or Not\par \par \uc2 In this debate about critical race theory, Mr. Kirk borrowed from the Socratic method, using questions to steer his opponent into a disadvantage. Flustered, the young person conceded ground, and he leaped to occupy it. ''This is great,'' he said before asking another question designed to frame the opposing position.\par \par \uc2 Kirk: Black people are only 13 percent of the population, yet they commit 55 percent of the murders. Why?\par \par \uc2 Opponent: Because Black people tend to be in lower economic statuses because of complica -- because of --\par \par \uc2 Kirk: OK, no, no, this is fine. This is great.\par \par \uc2 Opponent: -- C.R.T., basically.\par \par \uc2 Kirk: So you think that poverty equals crime.\par \par \uc2 It generated the response Mr. Kirk seems to have hoped for, and he pounced, accusing his opponent of insulting ''the working poor'' by suggesting there's a correlation between poverty and violent crime. Bill Southworth, a professor of speech and debate at the University of Redlands, described this approach as trying to ''trivialize'' his opponent as an out-of-touch member of the elite, while ''increasing his own ethos'' as the defender of regular, working-class people.\par \par \uc2 There is in fact extensive research suggesting a strong correlative relationship between poverty and crime. Studies show that relationship holds across demographic lines, and also that systemic inequities in the judicial system contribute to racial disparities in homicide rates.\par \par \uc2 Opponent: I think that it's highly correlated --\par \par \uc2 Kirk: Ah, this is where we disagree.\par \par \uc2 Opponent: -- and there's a lot of studies on that.\par \par \uc2 Kirk: What an insult to the working poor of this country.\par \par \uc2 Opponent: So why do you think Black people commit more crime, Charlie Kirk?\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kirk later tried another of his frequently used tactics, introducing a compelling statistic in order to demolish a claim about poverty and crime -- in this case, that 80 percent of Black people in America ''do not have a stable father around.'' The problem is that the stat, which he often cited in debates, is not accurate.\par \par \uc2 Kirk: So, how -- let me ask you a question as my answer.\par \par \uc2 Opponent: No, answer it. Go on.\par \par \uc2 Kirk: What percentage of Blacks have a father around when they're raised?\par \par \uc2 Opponent: I'm not sure.\par \par \uc2 Kirk: Twenty percent. Eighty percent of Blacks do not have a stable father around.\par \par \uc2 Opponent: So that's why you think it is --\par \par \uc2 Kirk: That is the most predictable way to end up in prison, end up as a murderer or a criminal.\par \par \uc2 We found at least four other times that Mr. Kirk used a variation of this inaccurate statistic in debates.\par \par \uc2 Tim Pool's podcast | Aug. 3, 2021 ''Right before the Civil Rights Act passed, about 24 percent of Black children were born without a father. Now it's upwards of 70 percent.''\par \par \uc2 Jubilee 1v25 debate | Sept. 8, 2024 ''Seventy-five percent of Black youth are not raised with a father in the home.''\par \par \uc2 University of Nevada, Reno\uc2  | Oct. 8, 2024 ''Today, it's nearly 75 percent, about 70 percent. So that means one in four Black kids will have a father around. Only one in four.''\par \par \uc2 University of Tennessee\uc2  | March 13, 2025 ''In 1964, right before the passage of the Civil Rights Act, 75 percent of Black youth had a dad around. Now it's 25 percent.''\par \par \uc2 Studies by the federal government and nonprofit groups show that slightly less than half of Black children live without a father at home as of 2023, and historically that number has not risen above about 65 percent. It also does not account for fathers who do not live with their children -- after a divorce, for instance -- but may otherwise be a regular presence in their lives.\par \par \uc2 But because his opponent was unable to fact check Mr. Kirk in real time, she was forced to concede and debate in a framework that was no longer grounded in reality.\par \par \uc2 Sept. 5, 2025\par \par \uc2 University of Wisconsin\uc2 , Madison\par \par \uc2 Unprovable Generalizations\par \par \uc2 ''I'll prove it to you,'' Mr. Kirk frequently offered in debates. What followed did not always deliver on that promise. Here, he made some categorically unprovable assertions about the differences between women and men, including what women discuss when they have lunch.\par \par \uc2 By playing up stereotypes, Mr. Kirk was once again pushing emotional buttons. He also may have been trying to overwhelm his opponent with what Carl Trigilio, who has coached championship debaters at the high school and college levels, calls ''sensory overload.'' The tactic can be successful because it is challenging to rebut every point and forces opponents to passively concede points.\par \par \uc2 Kirk: Will women be as good at engineering as men, person for person?\par \par \uc2 Student: Ah, not necessarily, but --\par \par \uc2 Kirk: OK, well then how is that different than the Secret Service example?\par \par \uc2 Student: Well, the problem is that, for example --\par \par \uc2 Kirk: Because the male mind is wired differently than the female mind. By the way, men will never be as good -- hold on, I'll prove it to you. Men will never be as good at counseling or psychology sessions as women. Men will never be good at nursing or elementary school education as women. We have certain giftings as our brain is wired a certain way. Women are far better gifted at micro topics, so individual connection, empathetic type conversations, healing people that have past trauma and wounds. Men are much better at macro topics, right? Trying to build a building, put a skyscraper up, trying to talk geopolitics, and I'll prove it to you. When you go and you see five women eating lunch, they're probably talking about micro topics, conversations they had, relationships, friendships. Men, they're talking about politics, sports or the stock market.\par \par \uc2 Spinning up millions of views\par \par \uc2 Skills Honed Over Time\par \par \uc2 In the most viral videos of Mr. Kirk's debates on social media, he looks strikingly composed, armed with enough rhetorical weapons and poise that he seems to never ''lose.'' But his videos were not always so predictable.\par \par \uc2 Toward the end of a 2018 debate with the left-wing pundit Hasan Piker, he jumped to his feet to yell at Cenk Uygur, another progressive commentator (and Mr. Piker's uncle) who appeared to be heckling him from the crowd. ''Come on, Cenk. Let's go!'' he said, seeming to challenge the man to a fight.\par \par \uc2 Kirk: I live like a capitalist every single day, Cenk.\par \par \uc2 Voice: Hey! Guys! Hey, hey, hey!\par \par \uc2 Kirk: I live as a capitalist, OK? I live what I believe. What do I do? I give to charity every single year.\par \par \uc2 Voice: Hold on, hold on, hold on. Charlie, Charlie, Charlie, Charlie, take a seat.\par \par \uc2 Kirk: What's my salary? Less than his. Come on, Cenk. Let's go.\par \par \uc2 Over the next seven years, Mr. Kirk refined his debating skills considerably, picking up vast stores of confidence and keeping his composure.\par \par \uc2 That skill and polish stood out when taking on idealistic undergraduates with little to no debating experience, a core feature of most of his appearances. In a clip from 2025, Mr. Kirk answers the question, ''Why do you like Trump so much?'' by calmly and methodically overwhelming his opponent with a litany of memorized talking points delivered at high speed, a technique that Dr. Voth said tends to be ''connotative of intelligence'' to audiences.\par \par \uc2 Kirk: Border crossings are down 99.9 percent. Oil is down. Inflation is down, price of eggs is down. We're going to see peace between Russia and Ukraine. We're going to have a tax cut, no tax on tips, no tax on overtime, no men in female sports, no chemical castration. Drill, baby, drill. Next question. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Late last year, Mr. Kirk sat down for what would be one of his most viral debates, against what he described as ''20 woke college kids,'' in a staged event organized by the media company Jubilee. In a clip, Mr. Kirk can be seen at the top of his game, leading off by asking his opponent to define a woman while debating the topic ''trans women are not women.'' The full debate video has over 37 million views on Jubilee's \uc2 YouTube\uc2  channel and spun off countless highlight reels that laud Mr. Kirk as ''owning'' and ''destroying'' his liberal opponents.\par \par \uc2 Kirk: Let's define our terms. Ah, a woman is an adult female, XX chromosomes. You tell me: What is a woman?\par \par \uc2 Student: I think a woman is somebody who identifies as a woman.\par \par \uc2 Kirk: Got it. So that's not a definition. That's like saying a table is something that identifies as a table. Give me an objective definition of --\par \par \uc2 Student: No, it's not. A table is an object.\par \par \uc2 Kirk: Well, tell, tell --\par \par \uc2 Student: A table can't identify as anything. It doesn't have choice.\par \par \uc2 Kirk: You have to give me an objective definition of what a woman is.\par \par \uc2 Student: If I decided right now that I wanted to identify as a woman, I would be a woman.\par \par \uc2 Kirk: OK, but that's not -- that doesn't answer the question.\par \par \uc2 Student: I know.\par \par \uc2 At the moment he was shot, Mr. Kirk was about 20 minutes into another ''prove me wrong'' debate, parrying a question about gun violence with a diversionary response about gang violence.\par \par \uc2 It is, perhaps, a testament to Mr. Kirk's influence that Hunter Kozak, the liberal Utah Valley University student who asked that final question, occupied a part of the debate economy Mr. Kirk helped create.\par \par \uc2 Debate videos are a source of entertainment and information. Mr. Kozak has built a platform of over 40,000 followers on TikTok, in part by attending debates held by Mr. Kirk, where he interviewed attendees on topics like transgender rights.\par \par \uc2 After Mr. Kirk's death, his widow, Erika Kirk, was elected chair and chief executive of Turning Point USA, her husband'sorganization. It's not clear if Mrs. Kirk will carry on her husband's debating, but she will be on an 11-campus nationwide college tour.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251013elad0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
