\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 E.P.A. Is Asked To Detect Use Of  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pill\b0\par\par\uc2 By Caroline Kitchener and Coral Davenport \par \uc2 1,508 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 October 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Senior officials at the \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2  directed scientists over the summer to assess whether the government could develop methods for detecting traces of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills in wastewater -- a practice sought by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists seeking to restrict the medication.\par \par \uc2 The highly unusual request appears to have originated from a letter sent from 25 Republican members of Congress to Lee Zeldin, the E.P.A. administrator, asking the agency to investigate how the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drug mifepristone might be contaminating the water supply.\par \par \uc2 ''Are there existing E.P.A.-approved methods for detecting mifepristone and its active metabolites in water supplies?'' the lawmakers asked at the end of the public letter, sent on June 18, an effort led by Senator James Lankford and Representative Josh Brecheen, both of Oklahoma. ''If not, what resources are needed to develop these testing methods?''\par \par \uc2 Scientists who specialize in chemical detection told the senior officials that there are currently no E.P.A.-approved methods for identifying mifepristone in wastewater -- but that new methods could be developed, according to two people familiar with the events, who spoke on the condition of anonymity to discuss sensitive information.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills have emerged as a major focus for the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement since the fall of Roe v. Wade, as growing numbers of women in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans have turned to websites and underground networks that send the pills through the mail, allowing them to circumvent the laws.\par \par \uc2 The widespread availability of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills -- which women usually take at home in the first 10 weeks of pregnancy -- has inspired many anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists to push for new approaches to curtail their use. That has included a campaign by one prominent group to raise awareness about environmental harms they say are caused when the medication and fetal remains enter the sewage system.\par \par \uc2 The medication, now used in over 50 percent of abortions, essentially prompts a miscarriage, and women sometimes pass fetal remains into the toilet. There is no evidence that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills contaminate Americans' water supply, and environmental experts have dismissed such claims.\par \par \uc2 It is not clear what became of the E.P.A. scientists' review, though there is no sign that any methods are under development for detecting mifepristone in wastewater. A spokesman for Senator Lankford said the office had not received a response to the lawmakers' letter as of Friday. The E.P.A. press secretary, Brigit Hirsch, did not respond to a question about the status of the work inside the agency, saying in a statement that the E.P.A. was merely reacting to the lawmakers' inquiry.\par \par \uc2 ''E.P.A. is frequently asked questions by Congress and the agency does, in fact, do due diligence to try to locate answers and prepare replies,'' Ms. Hirsch said.\par \par \uc2 Some anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists have been frustrated by the lack of action on the issue by the Trump administration this year, with many surprised last week when the Food and Drug Administration approved a generic version of mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 But several former E.P.A. officials who had worked on water quality and chemical detection issues expressed concern upon learning that agency scientists had been asked to explore the potential for detecting the drug. Some said that the general technology they had developed for testing wastewater could be used for surveillance in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is illegal. In an extreme case, the people said, wastewater testing could help identify a particular street or home where the pills were used, though such measures would be legally fraught and extremely costly.\par \par \uc2 ''They could isolate it to an apartment building or a school or a public building,'' said Jennifer Orme-Zavaleta, a former top official at E.P.A.'s Office of Research and Development who worked at the agency for 40 years and left in 2021. ''Could they go so far as to isolate it from an individual house? It's unclear if that's legal.''\par \par \uc2 Legal experts who specialize in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  noted that wastewater testing technology could potentially be used to help surface cases necessary for enforcing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, allowing anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists and district attorneys to identify particular areas where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills have been used. Since the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans took effect, even the most conservative prosecutors have struggled to find specific instances of women receiving pills -- which they need in order to bring charges against doctors and others who help facilitate those abortions.\par \par \uc2 ''The enforcement struggle is real,'' said Mary Ziegler, a law professor at the \uc2 University of California, Davis\uc2 . ''The concerning thing about identifying mifepristone in wastewater is that it could potentially lead to a much more robust set of cases, which would lead to a much more robust set of prosecutions.''\par \par \uc2 When he heard what had been asked of his former colleagues, one scientist who had left the E.P.A. in the last year said he immediately questioned the potential consequences of the technology he had spent years developing. Speaking on condition of anonymity out of fear of retribution, he described a ''sinking feeling'' that he and his colleagues had ''accidentally built the Death Star.''\par \par \uc2 Leaders of Students for Life of America, a major anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group that helped organize the letter from Congress to the E.P.A., said the organization was not at all interested in using wastewater testing to trace mifepristone use back to particular women. Rather, the group said, the goal is to expose environmental harms it believes stem from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related waste.\par \par \uc2 ''Students for Life is not a prosecution-of-women organization,'' said Kristi Hamrick, the group's vice president of media and policy. ''That's not what we're looking for.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hamrick said she was thrilled that the E.P.A. had started looking into these questions. ''This is long overdue,'' she said, adding that the government had been ''absolutely negligent'' by granting what she called a ''de facto permit for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pollution.''\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for Mr. Lankford said that scientific research into the effects of mifepristone on the environment ''should not be controversial.''\par \par \uc2 ''While federal regulators are quick to study the impact of chemicals in our water and septic systems, they have ignored the environmental and public health consequences of widespread chemical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' the spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 It is appropriate and not uncommon for the E.P.A. to receive requests from Congress to conduct research on pollutants in water, said Elizabeth Southerland, a former official in the E.P.A.'s Office of Water who worked at the agency for 30 years and left in 2017. ''But this request is off the charts,'' Ms. Southerland said. ''We often got letters from congressional representatives asking for a new analytical method, but it was because they had a known situation of contamination or pollution.''\par \par \uc2 To identify chemicals in a water sample, scientists use a specialized tool to measure their mass -- a precise number that is typically shared by only a handful of chemicals out of millions of possibilities. This kind of wastewater surveillance technology has been used to assess how much fentanyl is circulating in a community. It was also used during the coronavirus pandemic to measure the spread of the virus -- with scientists able to pinpoint specific areas with high levels of infection.\par \par \uc2 Students for Life doubled down on a campaign that focused on wastewater several months after Roe was overturned. In several letters to government agencies, the organization said that trace amounts of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills and fetal remains in wastewater could arguably injure people, animals, and the environment. At a private meeting in November 2022, Students for Life employees expressed frustration that government officials were not screening wastewater for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, ultimately resolving to hire ''student investigators'' to test the water on their own.\par \par \uc2 The group is now pursuing a more extensive study on the topic, Ms. Hamrick said.\par \par \uc2 The campaign gained little traction under the Biden administration. In the spring of 2024, a letter from 11 members of Congress, including Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  of Florida, now the secretary of state, asked the E.P.A. administrator to answer a series of general questions on how the rising use of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills might affect the water supply.\par \par \uc2 When the request came in from Mr. Rubio's office, the E.P.A.'s Office of Research and Development did look into whether any previous research had been done on the matter and found that it had not, according to two former E.P.A. officials who spoke on condition of anonymity because of the sensitivity of the matter. The office did not initiate any new research into the topic, the two officials said.\par \par \uc2 This year's version of the letter was more pointed, going one step further and specifically asking what resources the E.P.A. would need to test for mifepristone in water.\par \par \uc2 ''We commend this administration's dedication to protecting life and safeguarding public health,'' the letter begins.\par \par \uc2 Kitty Bennett contributed research.\par \par \uc2 Kitty Bennett contributed research.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020251011elab0003k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
