\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECT\par \b \uc2 Texas Senate Passes Bill to Crack Down on Mail-Order  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pills\b0\par\par\uc2 By Yan Zhuang \par \uc2 497 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 September 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 The New York Times on the Web\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Gov. Greg Abbott is expected to sign the legislation, which allows nearly anyone to sue \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication providers and distributors.\par \par \uc2 The Texas State Senate on Wednesday passed a bill that would crack down on mail-order \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medications, allowing ordinary people to sue providers, distributors and manufacturers for damages.\par \par \uc2 The bill, which passed 17-8, now heads to the desk of Gov. Greg Abbott, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Republican, who is expected to sign it into law.\par \par \uc2 It would allow residents unconnected to a pregnant woman to sue medical providers, pharmaceutical companies and courier services like \uc2 FedEx\uc2  and UPS, which may ship \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication to Texans, or intend to do so, for damages. Women who take such medications to end their pregnancies cannot be sued under the legislation. A suit can be brought even if no \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has taken place.\par \par \uc2 The Texas legislation is part of a wider campaign against the increasing popularity of medical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists want to expand limits on the procedure into states that ostensibly protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, like New York and California.\par \par \uc2 The bill's supporters hope that it will serve as a model for other states to limit access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication by prompting a rash of lawsuits. Its backers also hope that it would undermine ''shield laws'' in Democrat-led states that protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers from liability.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents have said that the legislation could limit the availability of medical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  even in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains legal, because manufacturers and delivery companies may limit distribution in Texas to avoid legal liability.\par \par \uc2 John Seago, the president of Texas Right to Life, described the bill as ''the most effective Pro-Life defense against out-of-state companies and activists that send \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to Texas.''\par \par \uc2 ''Today, our law became a blueprint for the rest of the country,'' Mr. Seago said in a statement after the bill's passage in the Texas Senate.\par \par \uc2 The bill's opponents fear how the legislation could be used.\par \par \uc2 ''It deputizes Texans as bounty hunters,'' said Carol Alvarado, a Democratic senator, during the debate late Wednesday. ''It turns Texas into a state of surveillance, not safety, and it exports Texas law across state lines.\par \par \uc2 The number of abortions nationwide has not declined since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade in 2022 and a raft of conservative states banned the procedure. Studies have found that a key reason is a rise in abortions using prescription pills, mifepristone and misoprostol, which are often prescribed and mailed from states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal.\par \par \uc2 The legislation is Texan lawmakers' second attempt to try to curb mail-order \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills. They failed to pass a similar legislation in the spring.\par \par \uc2 It was already illegal to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication in Texas under the state's near-total ban, which comes with serious criminal and civil penalties.\par \par \uc2 David Goodman contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 David Goodman contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250904el940004q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
