\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 In Lawsuit, Woman Claims a Marine Spiked Her Drink With  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pills\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alexandra E. Petri \par \uc2 958 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 August 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A Texas woman said in a lawsuit that a U.S. Marine got her pregnant and then, after she chose to keep the pregnancy, dissolved \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills in her hot chocolate.\par \par \uc2 A Texas woman accused a U.S. Marine in a lawsuit on Monday of lacing her drink with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, terminating her pregnancy against her wishes.\par \par \uc2 The woman, Liana Davis, 37, claims that Christopher Cooprider, 34, a U.S. Marine Corps Captain from Arizona who she says impregnated her, secretly dissolved 10 pills of misoprostol, a drug used in medical abortions, in a hot chocolate he made for her on April 5.\par \par \uc2 She made the accusation in a civil complaint filed in a federal court in Texas.\par \par \uc2 Shortly after drinking the beverage, the complaint says, Ms. Davis began bleeding and went to the hospital, where she lost her pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit also names Aid Access, a major seller of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills online, as a defendant. Ms. Davis said in the lawsuit that Mr. Cooprider used Aid Access to obtain the pills. The suit named the founder of Aid Access, the Dutch physician Rebecca Gomperts, as a third defendant.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cooprider, a flight student with the U.S. Marines in Corpus Christi, Texas, declined to comment on the lawsuit or say if he had a lawyer.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Marine Corps\uc2  said in a statement that it was aware of a civil lawsuit involving one of its aviation students but that no additional information was available.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Dr. Gomperts pointed to Aid Access's role in providing ''essential, lifesaving health care'' for vulnerable women.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Davis became pregnant by Mr. Cooprider in early 2025, the lawsuit claims. Text messages included in the lawsuit and said to be from Ms. Davis to Mr. Cooprider describe the pregnancy as ''unplanned.'' In one message she called herself Mr. Cooprider's ''mistress.''\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit says that Ms. Davis has three children with her husband, with whom she was in divorce proceedings.\par \par \uc2 According to the messages, Mr. Cooprider told Ms. Davis that he wanted her to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even before she had confirmed her pregnancy with a test.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Davis texted Mr. Cooprider on Jan. 31, 2025, for his thoughts on her possible pregnancy, and he responded, ''Get rid of it,'' the lawsuit said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cooprider added that the two were not in love or a couple, and that bringing a child into the world would be ''messed up,'' according to the lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 On Feb. 3, Mr. Cooprider sent a text message saying that if the pregnancy test was ''positive then we need to schedule a clinic visit and get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill asap,'' according to the complaint.\par \par \uc2 On Feb. 5, Mr. Cooprider texted Ms. Davis and said he was going to order \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills for her, according to the complaint, a step it said Ms. Davis opposed. On Feb. 6, Mr. Cooprider purchased pills from Aid Access, according to the lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm not OK with you buying something like that w/o my permission,'' Ms. Davis wrote him, according to the complaint.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cooprider brought the pills to Ms. Davis's home and repeatedly asked her to take them, according to the lawsuit. Pictures included in the lawsuit show two containers of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drugs labeled ''Chris Cooprider.''\par \par \uc2 The text exchanges grew more heated over several weeks, then took an abrupt turn. In early April, the lawsuit says, Mr. Cooprider texted Ms. Davis to propose a ''trust building night'' where he would make them ''warm relaxing tea.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cooprider went to Ms. Davis's home on April 5, where he made her a hot chocolate, according to the lawsuit. Ms. Davis was eight weeks pregnant at the time, the lawsuit said.\par \par \uc2 According to the lawsuit, she began hemorrhaging and cramping within 30 minutes of drinking the beverage.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cooprider said he would drive to get Ms. Davis's mother, who lived nearby, the lawsuit said. The plan was that she would mind Ms. Davis's three children, and Mr. Cooprider could then drive Ms. Davis to the emergency room.\par \par \uc2 But after leaving to get Ms. Davis's mother around 12:10 a.m., Mr. Cooprider stopped responding to calls and texts, and did not pick her up, according to the lawsuit. Screenshots of text messages included in the lawsuit show Ms. Davis repeatedly texting Mr. Cooprider, including one sent at 12:34 a.m. saying: ''I'm gushing blood. Please hurry.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Davis sent her mother money for an \uc2 Uber\uc2  just before 1 a.m., around the same time Mr. Cooprider responded to the text messages, the lawsuit said. He apologized and told her he had a flight in the morning, according to the complaint, and then stopped answering.\par \par \uc2 Before leaving the house, Ms. Davis found the opened packets of pills, the lawsuit said.\par \par \uc2 A neighbor drove Ms. Davis to a Bay Area hospital's emergency room, where she lost the pregnancy, according to the complaint.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Davis brought the pill bottles with her to the emergency room and gave them to the Corpus Christi Police Department, according to the lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 The Police Department said a detective investigated the allegations and shared the results with the Nueces County District Attorney's Office.\par \par \uc2 ''After careful review, both agencies concluded that the elements of a crime could not be established, and the investigation was subsequently closed as unfounded,'' Madeline Vaughn, a spokeswoman with the department, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Davis's suit seeks punitive damages from the defendants. It did not give a figure, but said the amount was greater than $75,000.\par \par \uc2 Sheelagh McNeill contributed research.\par \par \uc2 Sheelagh McNeill contributed research.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250818el8i00030\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
