\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Injunction Frees Clinics To Receive Medicaid\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Benner \par \uc2 697 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 July 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The nonprofit is challenging a new law that bars its clinics from receiving federal money for any treatment, including birth control and checkups.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  won a temporary injunction on Monday that allows its clinics to continue to receive Medicaid funding for services that are unrelated to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The organization sued the Trump administration earlier on Monday over a new law that essentially bars \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  clinics from receiving federal Medicaid payments, claiming that the legislation is an unconstitutional attack on \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 's national organization and its locally run health care clinics.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit, which was filed in Federal District Court in Massachusetts, challenges part of the new domestic policy bill that President Trump signed on Friday. Judge Indira Talwani issued the temporary injunction, which expires in 14 days.\par \par \uc2 Under the new law, some nonprofit health centers that provide abortions cannot be reimbursed by Medicaid for providing any other medical services, including birth control, annual checkups and tests for sexually transmitted infections. (The use of federal Medicaid dollars to cover the cost of abortions has long been illegal.)\par \par \uc2 The new law applies only to nonprofit organizations that generated $800,000 or more in revenue from Medicaid payments in the 2023 fiscal year. Because few \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers are large enough to meet that threshold, the lawsuit argues that the law is intended to target \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  for its advocacy of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, violating the group's freedom of speech.\par \par \uc2 ''The Defund Provision is a naked attempt to leverage the government's spending power to attack and penalize \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  and impermissibly single it out for unfavorable treatment,'' \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  said in its lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 ''It does so not only because of Planned Parenthood Members' long history of providing legal abortions to patients across the country, but also because of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 's unique role in advocating for policies to protect and expand access to sexual and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' the lawsuit said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2 , which administers Medicaid, did not respond immediately to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 After the new law was enacted, some \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  affiliates scrambled to let patients know on Friday that they could no longer use their federal health care coverage to pay their bills.\par \par \uc2 ''It was devastating to have to tell patients that we could no longer bill Medicaid and see them realize that they wouldn't be able to come back to us for their next appointment,'' Dr. Luu D. Ireland, the chief medical officer at Planned Parenthood League of Massachusetts, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Each year, more than one million patients in 43 states receive health care services at \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  that are covered by Medicaid.\par \par \uc2 The clinics are operated by 47 stand-alone nonprofits called affiliates, some of which rely on Medicaid for over three quarters of their revenue.\par \par \uc2 Stripping Planned Parenthood clinics of those patients and their Medicaid reimbursements ''will result in the elimination of services, laying off of staff, and health center closures,'' according to the lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood Federation of America\uc2  sued on behalf of itself and all 47 of its members, including two affiliates named in the complaint, Planned Parenthood League of Massachusetts and Planned Parenthood Association of Utah.\par \par \uc2 Both affiliates, which operate in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal, receive Medicaid reimbursement for other medical treatment, as do independent clinics that have no association with \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The new federal law's $800,000 threshold essentially allows these smaller independent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers to continue receiving federal reimbursements, even as the law bans \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  affiliates from federal funding.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  said in its lawsuit that the law's focus on punishing \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  health centers violates their First Amendment right to free speech.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit also said that Utah received $706,251 in Medicaid reimbursements in the federal fiscal year 2023, just below the $800,000 threshold in the law.\par \par \uc2 If a judge chooses not to rule that the new law violates the First Amendment, the decision could find that a state like Utah is exempt from the ban because it does not meet the $800,000 threshold.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250709el790003i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
