\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court Says Planned Parenthood Can't Sue South Carolina for Defunding\b0\par\par\uc2 By Adam Liptak \par \uc2 1,010 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The question for the justices was whether Medicaid beneficiaries may sue to receive services under a law that lets them choose any qualified provider.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court ruled on Thursday that \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  and one of its patients cannot sue South Carolina over its effort to deny funding to the group, reasoning that the relevant federal statute does not authorize such suits.\par \par \uc2 The vote was 6 to 3, with the court's three liberal members in dissent.\par \par \uc2 In 2018, Gov. \uc2 Henry McMaster\uc2  of South Carolina, a Republican, ordered state officials to deny Medicaid funds to \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , saying that ''payment of taxpayer funds to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics, for any purpose, results in the subsidy of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the denial of the right to life.''\par \par \uc2 But \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was mentioned only in passing in the decision, and the patient in the case had sought access to contraception, not an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Instead, the justices focused on whether the plaintiffs were entitled to sue to enforce part of the Medicaid law, which gives federal money to states to provide medical care for poor people.\par \par \uc2 Still, in shutting down such suits in federal court, the majority made it easier for states to deny funding to \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , particularly given the current administration's hostility to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Medicaid provides federal money to states, but it sets some conditions. One is that eligible participants may receive assistance from any provider qualified to perform the required services. At issue in the case was whether a patient could sue to enforce that provision and obtain \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  services for medical treatment other than abortions.\par \par \uc2 Abortions are banned in South Carolina after six weeks of pregnancy. Even then, federal law prohibits the use of Medicaid funding for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  except in life-threatening circumstances or in cases of rape or incest. But \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  clinics in Charleston and Columbia provide services unrelated to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including counseling, physical exams, contraception and screenings for cancer and sexually transmitted infections.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Neil M. Gorsuch\uc2 , writing for the majority, said private suits to enforce federal statutes are rarely permissible and require clear congressional authorization.\par \par \uc2 ''After all, the decision whether to let private plaintiffs enforce a new statutory right poses delicate questions of public policy,'' he wrote, adding that those questions should be resolved by ''the people's elected representatives, not unelected judges.''\par \par \uc2 Justice Gorsuch wrote that there are other ways to enforce the statute. The federal government can cut off Medicaid money, he wrote, and South Carolina has an administrative process in which medical providers can challenge their exclusion from the state's Medicaid program.\par \par \uc2 In dissent, Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 , joined by Justices \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  and \uc2 Elena Kagan\uc2 , said the law in question directly authorized the suit.\par \par \uc2 She added that Justice Gorsuch's proposed alternatives were unrealistic. ''In practice,'' she wrote, the federal government ''rarely invokes its authority to withhold funding because doing so would inevitably harm the program's beneficiaries.''\par \par \uc2 Justice Jackson concluded her dissent by predicting that grave consequences would flow from the majority's approach.\par \par \uc2 ''Today's decision is likely to result in tangible harm to real people,'' she wrote. ''At a minimum, it will deprive Medicaid recipients in South Carolina of their only meaningful way of enforcing a right that Congress has expressly granted to them. And, more concretely, it will strip those South Carolinians -- and countless other Medicaid recipients around the country -- of a deeply personal freedom: the 'ability to decide who treats us at our most vulnerable.'''\par \par \uc2 John J. Bursch\uc2 , a lawyer with the \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2 , a conservative Christian group that represented South Carolina, welcomed the ruling.\par \par \uc2 ''The American people don't want their tax dollars propping up the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  industry,'' he said in a statement. ''The Supreme Court rightly restored the ability of states like South Carolina to steward limited public resources to best serve their citizens.''\par \par \uc2 Paige Johnson\uc2 , president of Planned Parenthood South Atlantic, which brought the suit, said in a statement, ''Today's decision is a grave injustice that strikes at the very bedrock of American freedom and promises to send South Carolina deeper into a health care crisis.''\par \par \uc2 She added that the South Carolina law was an effort to ''weaponize anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  sentiment to deprive communities with low incomes of basic health care.''\par \par \uc2 A federal trial judge blocked the South Carolina directive, saying that it ran afoul of Medicaid's requirement that patients may choose any qualified provider.\par \par \uc2 The litigation that followed was convoluted and circuitous, focusing largely on whether Medicaid's provision created a right that individuals could enforce by filing lawsuits. The Supreme Court has said that federal laws like Medicaid, which give money to states but only if they accept certain conditions, must ''unambiguously confer individual federal rights'' to give affected individuals the right to sue.\par \par \uc2 That is a hard test to meet, and the court has only rarely ruled that it has been satisfied, most recently in 2023 in Health and Hospital Corporation of Marion County v. Talevski, a case concerning nursing homes. The statute at issue in that case repeatedly referred to ''rights'' as such, while the Medicaid provision in the new case, Medina v. Planned Parenthood South Atlantic, No. 23-1275, uses different language.\par \par \uc2 That law says that people seeking medical services ''may obtain such assistance from any institution'' that is ''qualified to perform the service or services required.''\par \par \uc2 Last year, a unanimous three-judge panel of the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit, in Richmond, Va., ruled that the suit could proceed.\par \par \uc2 ''This case is, and always has been, about whether Congress conferred an individually enforceable right for Medicaid beneficiaries to freely choose their health care provider,'' Judge J. Harvie Wilkinson III wrote for the panel. ''Preserving access to \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  and other providers means preserving an affordable choice and quality care for an untold number of mothers and infants in South Carolina.''\par \par \uc2 He added that ''this decision is not about funding or providing abortions.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250627el6r0003z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
