\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Lawmakers Vote to Decriminalize Abortions for Women in England and Wales\b0\par\par\uc2 By Stephen Castle \par \uc2 843 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 June 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The landmark decision will prevent women from being criminally prosecuted for ending their pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 British lawmakers voted on Tuesday to prevent women from being prosecuted for having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in England and Wales, a landmark decision that sets the country on a diverging path from the United States on an emotive social issue.\par \par \uc2 In effect, the vote will largely decriminalize terminating pregnancies that are later than 24 weeks, the current limit, although medical professionals who aid the process could still be prosecuted.\par \par \uc2 Britain's Parliament\uc2 , by a vote of 370 to 137, approved an amendment to a bill that would change Victorian-era laws governing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The vote comes at a time of intense debate over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the United States, where the Supreme Court overturned the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2022 and threw it to the 50 states to legislate their own laws.\par \par \uc2 By contrast, surveys suggest that the vast majority of the British population -- consistently around 88 percent, according to \uc2 YouGov\uc2 , a polling agency -- say they believe that women should have the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Was \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  already legal in Britain?\par \par \uc2 It's complicated. Currently, having or providing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains a crime under the Offenses Against the Person Act of 1861 and could, theoretically, be punished with a life sentence.\par \par \uc2 But the \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Act of 1967 made it legal to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in England, Scotland and Wales, initially up to the first 28 weeks of pregnancy; that period was reduced to 24 weeks in 1990.\par \par \uc2 Certain conditions must be met:\par \par \uc2 Two doctors must authorize the decision.\par \par \uc2 Continuing the pregnancy would involve risk to the woman's mental or physical health.\par \par \uc2 An \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 24 weeks is allowed only if:\par \par \uc2 There is a risk to the mother's life, or of grave permanent injury to her health\par \par \uc2 There is a substantial risk of a serious fetal anomaly.\par \par \uc2 Why do some believe the law needed to change?\par \par \uc2 Campaigners for change argue that the 1861 law is outdated and that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be regulated as a health care issue rather than a possible crime.\par \par \uc2 There is also concern that during the past three years, six women have been charged with ending or trying to end their pregnancy outside \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law.\par \par \uc2 Over the past decade, the percentage of abortions performed after 20 weeks has remained extremely low -- below 2 percent. Campaigners for change argue that those who take \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills past the cutoff point are often victims of domestic abuse, have mental health problems or are teenagers whose parents are opposed to abortions, and that they should receive health care and other services rather than face prison time.\par \par \uc2 Changes to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the United States have also had an impact. Louise McCudden, head of external affairs at \uc2 MSI Reproductive Choices\uc2 , an international organization that provides family planning and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services, said in a statement: ''With the anti-rights movement feeling emboldened since the reversal of Roe v. Wade in the United States, it could not be more important that here in the U.K. elected lawmakers stand up for women and support everyone to get the medical care they need.''\par \par \uc2 How will the amendment change the law?\par \par \uc2 The amendment, which was proposed by Tonia Antoniazzi, a lawmaker for the governing Labour Party, will not repeal the existing criminal law, but aims to prevent women from being investigated, arrested, prosecuted or imprisoned for terminating their own pregnancies. Ms. Antoniazzi argues that those investigated can be victims of domestic abuse or sexual exploitation, or women who have given birth prematurely.\par \par \uc2 Another amendment that would have required a pregnant woman to have an in-person consultation before being prescribed pills to terminate a pregnancy was defeated.\par \par \uc2 Who opposes the proposed changes and who is in favor?\par \par \uc2 The Society for the Protection of Unborn Children, which campaigns against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has described the effort to change the law as ''the greatest threat to unborn children and their mothers since the 1967 \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Act.'' The organization says that both amendments would mean that no abortions up to birth in any circumstances would be criminalized.\par \par \uc2 Rebecca Smith, a Conservative lawmaker, told Parliament that supporters of the change ''claim that the 24-week time limit for abortions would not change, but in my view this is misleading.''\par \par \uc2 She added: ''Once decriminalization takes place, it is my concern that further steps will be taken to expand \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  time limits.''\par \par \uc2 But Ms. Antoniazzi's amendment was supported by the Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, the Faculty of Sexual and Reproductive Healthcare and the \uc2 British Pregnancy Advisory Service\uc2 , which said in a joint statement that decriminalization would give women in England and Wales the same protection from prosecution available to women in Northern Ireland, Ireland, France, Canada, Australia and New Zealand.\par \par \uc2 Ranee Thakar, president of the Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, said in a statement that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ''should be subject to regulatory and professional standards like other medical procedures, not criminal sanctions.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250618el6i0004e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
