\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Calm Voice on Phone, and  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pills by Mail\b0\par\par\uc2 By Pam Belluck and Hannah Yoon \par \uc2 2,782 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 June 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The young woman's voice trembled over the phone. Sitting in her car in Alabama, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is almost totally banned, the 26-year-old mother of two was grappling with an unintended pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm like 'How in the world?''' she said, stifling a sob. ''I already have two children, and I cannot. I can't. I just can't go through with it.''\par \par \uc2 She wanted an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , she said, but was afraid of getting caught and didn't know what to expect from the process. ''Growing up, I never really thought about actually doing something like this,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 On the other end of the line, at home on a quiet residential street in Delaware, Debra Lynch, a nurse practitioner who runs a service prescribing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, spoke calmly.\par \par \uc2 ''It's completely valid to be scared,'' she said from her desk in a home office filled with plants and shelves of medication. ''And that's why we want you to call us, even if you're calling just to say: 'I'm scared. I need to hear somebody tell me that what's going on right now is normal, and it's OK.'''\par \par \uc2 During the 25-minute conversation, Ms. Lynch asked the woman about her health history and pregnancy and assessed that she was medically eligible for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medications that can be taken in the first 12 weeks of pregnancy: mifepristone, which blocks a hormone necessary for pregnancy development, and misoprostol, taken 24 to 48 hours later, which causes contractions so pregnancy tissue can be expelled.\par \par \uc2 She carefully explained how to take them and mentioned that after the second medication, there would be cramping and bleeding that could continue for days.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Lynch's husband, Jay, packaged the pills into a plain white envelope and labeled it with the Alabama address, as well as their service's name and return address. A mail carrier picked it up from their mailbox. Included was a handwritten note on paper decorated with flowers: ''We are here for you if you need us. You are not alone. Feel free to reach out anytime, no matter what you need.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Lynch is one of about several dozen providers in the country taking legal risks by prescribing and sending pills to patients in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. Many providers are based in states with shield laws, intended to offer them protection by preventing authorities there from cooperating with out-of-state officials who try to prosecute or sue them for serving people in their states.\par \par \uc2 About 20 states have adopted some type of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  shield law since the Supreme Court overturned the national right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2022. Eight explicitly protect telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  prescribers who send medication to patients in any state. Delaware's shield law isn't as explicit, and there are different views on the scope of its protection, some legal experts said. Ms. Lynch said lawyers advised her that Delaware's laws appear to protect prescribers who mail pills to any state, but she recently decided to move to one of the eight states with the clearest protections.\par \par \uc2 The mailing of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills has become a major issue for anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists. In a lawsuit against the Food and Drug Administration, three Republican state attorneys general are seeking to reinstate rules requiring patients to obtain pills from providers in person. And \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents are pressing for other state and federal actions to curtail the sending of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication into states with bans.\par \par \uc2 ''It is violating not only our pro-life laws but our homicide laws,'' said John Seago, president of Texas Right to Life.\par \par \uc2 He added: ''We're really shocked that there's been a widespread embrace of this. And so for Texas, we've established it's immoral, it's unethical. We want to stop it.''\par \par \uc2 Shield laws have become a key \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights strategy, and each month, prescribers are sending medication to about 10,000 patients in states with bans. But the laws are beginning to be tested as authorities in states that outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bring legal action against such prescribers, a confrontation many expect to reach the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 The first cases -- a criminal indictment in Louisiana and a civil suit by the Texas attorney general -- involve a New York doctor accused of sending \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to those states. New York officials have refused to cooperate, invoking that state's shield law. But the cases have transformed the risk for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers from theoretical to real.\par \par \uc2 Given the stakes, most prescribers sending pills to states with bans keep their names and other identifying information out of public view. Ms. Lynch was willing to be named, saying that to ''step forward and identify who you are as an actual real live human'' might help some women needing abortions feel less fearful.\par \par \uc2 She allowed \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  to spend a day with her as she had phone consultations with patients. (The Alabama woman and others allowed The Times to listen; to protect their identity, The Times agreed not to name the patients.)\par \par \uc2 The visit offered a rare look at the work of one unconventional prescriber and the delicate and complex circumstances women seeking abortions may experience.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Lynch operates the service, called Her Safe Harbor, with three other volunteer licensed prescribers and Mr. Lynch, who handles various operational responsibilities and formerly worked for \uc2 Delaware's health and social services\uc2  department. The service, which started last June, also provides contraceptive pills and treatment for gynecological infections. The Lynches said the service ships several hundred packages a month, mailing to any address patients request, including a general store in a Midwestern town.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Lynch's medical guidance follows what most medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers recommend. But some other steps she takes push the envelope in ways other prescribers do not. Those steps, she said, are intended to reach patients who are especially concerned about privacy or nervous about the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  process.\par \par \uc2 She says she believes the risks she is taking pale in comparison to the risks patients take in seeking abortions. ''They are the ones who are really being brave, you know?'' she said.\par \par \uc2 A Call From Texas\par \par \uc2 There were cries of young children in the background as a mother of two in Texas described over the phone how she learned during a routine gynecologist appointment that she was pregnant again. She told Ms. Lynch that she didn't want her husband to know because he had sometimes been abusive. She asked that the pills be mailed to a friend's house, where she planned to take them while her husband was at work.\par \par \uc2 There was another issue though: How would the woman explain to her doctor why she was no longer pregnant? She told Ms. Lynch that she thought that she should visit an emergency room after taking the pills, so a hospital could document that she had a miscarriage. But she was terrified about whether \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills or even the nausea medicine that the service sends in the package could be detected with blood tests. She asked if she could tell the hospital not to take blood.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Lynch told her that standard blood and urine tests don't detect those medications and advised that saying she was having a miscarriage but didn't want lab tests could raise suspicion and impede the hospital's ability to provide the miscarriage documentation she wanted. After the call, she said the woman seemed reassured about what to do.\par \par \uc2 Many callers are in sensitive circumstances, Ms. Lynch said, including women who have been victims of date rape. She said concern for their safety and privacy was one reason she had adopted some practices that differ from other services.\par \par \uc2 ''It's not just obfuscation for the sake of obfuscation from law enforcement,'' she said. ''A lot of times, it's because it's a domestic violence situation or a high-risk-for-violence-in-the-home situation, or they live with other people who might out them.''\par \par \uc2 Women in states with bans have limited options for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They can travel to states with legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but that can be costly and involve time away from jobs and children. Some obtain pills from informal community networks that don't have medical professionals or prescription medication.\par \par \uc2 Many women choose another option: telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services that mail prescribed pills. Such prescribers often assess medical eligibility by reviewing forms that patients complete online, a system many patients consider convenient and efficient.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Lynch says her service works differently. It is designed for patients in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans and restrictions who want to talk with a provider on the phone or who worry that online forms might leave an electronic footprint, she said.\par \par \uc2 Typically, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill prescribers strictly comply with the laws of the state they're licensed in, which helps ensure that their state's shield law will protect them. For example, they carefully obey their state's requirements about sending the prescriptions with the medication. Ms. Lynch, however, said that to better serve patients who are afraid to receive such documentation, she decided not to put copies of the prescriptions in the packages, although such a practice would trouble the providers who follow the rules.\par \par \uc2 ''One of the main points that we heard from people was that they don't want a prescription with their name on it,'' she said. ''So, we had to make a decision: Are we willing to potentially violate a Delaware law with the labeling of the prescriptions in order to remove this barrier that's a very real barrier for a lot of people?''\par \par \uc2 Her service keeps prescriptions and other records for patients in paper files offsite, she said. To give patients additional ''plausible deniability,'' she said, she sends receipts with a medical code for a urinary tract infection consultation, one of the conditions the service treats, along with written information about U.T.I.s. She doesn't ask patients in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans or restrictions to provide identification like a driver's license.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Lynch, 56, has had an eclectic career and said she previously worked in geriatrics, chronic disease and other fields. Assisting a community Covid response team in Philadelphia ''kind of redirected my career focus on being more social-needs-oriented,'' she said. After Roe v. Wade was overturned, she wanted to offer support to women seeking abortions and admired the shield-law providers' work, she said.\par \par \uc2 A Queens native, she is voluble and expressive. During the recent visit, she was wearing a long blue floral dress and pink head scarf and was barefoot with a flowery vine tattoo spiraling down one leg. She and Mr. Lynch, a 61-year-old Brooklyn native, have been married for over 30 years and have worked together before, including once running a children's theater.\par \par \uc2 During phone consultations, Ms. Lynch's questions mirror the online forms other services use and her responses generally echo those of other providers. For example, she won't prescribe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to women with bleeding disorders or ectopic pregnancies, in which the fertilized egg is outside the uterus and never produces a baby.\par \par \uc2 Some patients ask what they should do if they want or need to visit an emergency room. Serious complications from medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are rare, and numerous studies have found it to be safe, including when pills are prescribed by telemedicine and mailed. Long before the F.D.A.'s 2021 decision permitting telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the agency considered the medication safe enough to allow patients to take it at home and not in the presence of a doctor. But some women want a hospital to assess whether their bleeding level is normal or whether all the pregnancy tissue has been passed.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Lynch, like other \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, counsels that there is no medical reason for women to tell hospitals they have taken \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, and that they can allow hospitals to assume they are miscarrying, which involves the same symptoms and is often treated with the same medications.\par \par \uc2 Her service often conducts follow-up calls, checking on patients after they take the medication and sometimes for days afterward. On four occasions, she has suggested that a patient visit an emergency room, she said. One woman was dehydrated, and two wondered if they were bleeding excessively. She wanted the fourth to be evaluated because of heavy bleeding. All turned out to be fine and needed no treatment at the hospital, she said.\par \par \uc2 A 'Pro-Life' Caller\par \par \uc2 After another woman in Texas had a consultation with Ms. Lynch and took the medication, the woman and her husband wanted to check that the process was progressing normally. The patient's husband called and texted several times a day, sometimes late at night.\par \par \uc2 The man said they were devout Christians who considered themselves ''pro-life'' but found themselves in circumstances where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was right for them. ''It's not very common that some grew-up-in-the-country Republican from Texas who loves guns changes his mind on things,'' he said on one call. ''But here we are.''\par \par \uc2 His wife has endometriosis and had been advised that pregnancy could be dangerous for her, he said. They worried that Texas' \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban made hospitals so afraid that if she miscarried or had pregnancy complications, doctors would have to wait to intervene until her condition became life-threatening.\par \par \uc2 ''If you're a woman in Texas, and you're going through complications and a miscarriage,'' he said, ''it's going to be difficult for you to find treatment, and that's not OK. And as a Christian, I understand that these laws stem from Christian values. But the one thing that we never really discuss is a woman's health.''\par \par \uc2 Six days after his wife began the medication regimen, he called again, asking if they should be concerned that some bleeding was still occurring.\par \par \uc2 ''No fevers, right?'' Ms. Lynch asked.\par \par \uc2 ''No nausea, no fevers,'' he said, adding that his wife ''keeps bleeding and cramping, but it's not crazy excessive.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Lynch suggested the woman take an additional two misoprostol tablets, noting that some women need more than the initial four tablets to fully expel pregnancy tissue. If bleeding didn't lessen by the next day, she said, ''then I probably would want to get her an ultrasound.''\par \par \uc2 She quickly explained: ''Now, she wouldn't have to go to the emergency room or anything, because as long as she doesn't have a fever or any signs of infection or continuous bleeding, it wouldn't be an emergency. So we could arrange for her to have an ultrasound there, locally, done without it going in her chart, or actually without the provider even having her name or any information.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Lynch's service has contacts for medical practitioners in many states who will provide ultrasounds and other care, she said, absorbing the cost themselves, as long as they aren't violating that state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws.\par \par \uc2 After the additional misoprostol, the bleeding eased, making an ultrasound unnecessary.\par \par \uc2 Like several other telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services, Her Safe Harbor typically charges $150 per order but also accepts whatever patients can afford. ''Right now, I have like $40 on me, and I realize that's probably not enough for anything,'' the Alabama woman said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Lynch, who handles logistics like billing and answering the phone, sent her the medications for free.\par \par \uc2 The Lynches recently decided to move to New York, which has one of the strongest shield laws. They've chosen a rural upstate community, where they can afford property large enough for a small clinic adjacent to their home. Ms. Lynch plans to apply for the necessary state nursing licenses.\par \par \uc2 To comply with New York's law, some of her practices would most likely need to change. But she said she appreciated that New York recently added another layer of protection by allowing providers to send patients prescriptions with the medical practice's name instead of the provider's name.\par \par \uc2 She applauded the state's forceful response to the Texas and Louisiana cases. Gov. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  of New York has refused to extradite the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider, Dr. Margaret Carpenter, to Louisiana, and a county clerk blocked an attempt by Texas to enforce a $113,000 penalty against Dr. Carpenter.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Lynch said those actions sent a signal that ''no matter what, we are going to protect the patients and we are going to protect the provider.''\par \par \uc2 Susan C. Beachy contributed research.\par \par \uc2 Audio produced by Sarah Diamond.\par \par \uc2 Susan C. Beachy contributed research. Audio produced by Sarah Diamond.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250616el6g0000o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
