\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Four States Ask the F.D.A. to Lift Special Restrictions on an  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pill\b0\par\par\uc2 By Pam Belluck \par \uc2 984 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 June 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The states consider it a move to force the F.D.A. to review and acknowledge extensive research showing the pill's safety.\par \par \uc2 In a strategy aimed at countering efforts to further restrict the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill mifepristone, attorneys general of four states that support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights on Thursday asked the Food and Drug Administration to do the opposite and lift the most stringent remaining restrictions on the pill.\par \par \uc2 The petition filed by Massachusetts, New York, California and New Jersey might seem surprising given the opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  expressed by Trump administration officials. But the attorneys general consider it a move that would require the F.D.A. to acknowledge extensive scientific research that has consistently found mifepristone safe and effective, said an official with the Massachusetts attorney general's office who worked on the filing and asked not to be named in order to share background information. It would also prevent the F.D.A. from changing mifepristone regulations while the petition is pending.\par \par \uc2 The petition notes that at a May Senate hearing, Robert F. Kennedy Jr., the health and human services secretary, responded to questions by Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2 , Republican of Missouri, who opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , by saying he had ordered the F.D.A. to do a ''complete review'' of mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 ''The \uc2 FDA\uc2  must follow the science and lift these unnecessary barriers that put patients at risk and push providers out of care,'' Letitia James, the attorney general of New York, said after the petition was filed on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The F.D.A. is required to respond to the petition within 180 days by granting or denying the request, or saying it needs more time. In its responses, the agency must document its position, which could be useful in lawsuits, including one that the four states could file if their petition is denied.\par \par \uc2 Mifepristone, which blocks a hormone necessary for pregnancy development, was approved for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in America in 2000. The F.D.A. imposed an additional regulatory framework called Risk Evaluation and Mitigation Strategy, or REMS, on mifepristone. That framework has been used for only about 300 drugs, currently covering only about 60 medications.\par \par \uc2 Mifepristone is the first pill in the two-drug medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimen in the United States, used through 12 weeks of pregnancy. It is followed 24 to 48 hours later by misoprostol, which causes contractions like a miscarriage. Misoprostol can terminate pregnancies itself but hasn't generally been targeted by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents, perhaps because it isn't specifically approved for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and can treat several medical conditions, including ulcers.\par \par \uc2 In the last decade, the F.D.A. reviewed new data on mifepristone and lifted several restrictions, including a requirement that patients obtain it in person from a provider. That change, in 2021, allowed it to be prescribed by telemedicine and mailed to patients, expanding access to mifepristone, now used in nearly two-thirds of American abortions.\par \par \uc2 ''We're glad the F.D.A. will re-examine the data after the Biden administration fueled an unregulated drug market, stripping the crucial safeguard of in-person dispensing for these high-risk drugs.'' Katie Glenn Daniel, director of legal affairs for the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group SBA Pro-Life America, said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The states' filing, called a citizen petition, seeks a complete lifting of the REMS framework, saying that ''given mifepristone's well-established, 25-year safety record, F.D.A.'s current restrictions on mifepristone are no longer justified by science or law.'' Citing new studies, it says ''mifepristone's safety has remained stable even as its restrictions have been lessened'' and that continuing the restrictions ''cannot be squared with the F.D.A.'s lack of REMS programs on drugs that have significantly more risks than mifepristone.''\par \par \uc2 Recently, a conservative faith-based organization released a report saying that mifepristone is more dangerous than the vast majority of studies show. Some conservative Republicans have urged Mr. Kennedy and the F.D.A. commissioner, Dr. Marty Makary, to review the drug based on that report.\par \par \uc2 Medical experts in \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  point to flaws in the report, including that it was not peer-reviewed and doesn't disclose the source of the insurance claims data it analyzed. The report also uses atypical methods to characterize what it calls ''serious adverse events,'' making it unclear, for example, whether an emergency room visit involved complications from mifepristone or other, more common issues that were either not serious or were unrelated to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  experts say.\par \par \uc2 Asked about the report at a recent hearing, Dr. Makary said ''there is no peer-reviewed publication, and the underlying data set is not available, but when it does become available, we're going to take a hard look at it.'' In a written response to Senator Hawley, Dr. Makary said he was ''committed to conducting a review of mifepristone and working with the professional career scientists at the agency who review this data.''\par \par \uc2 The petition requests that if the F.D.A. declines to lift the REMS, it ''exercise its discretion not to enforce'' some or all of those restrictions in the four states because their regulations are sufficient.\par \par \uc2 The REMS provisions still in place include special certification processes for prescribers and for pharmacies dispensing mifepristone, requirements the petition says are unnecessary and have discouraged some providers from offering the medication.\par \par \uc2 Another requirement is an agreement patients must sign saying they are taking mifepristone because they decided to terminate their pregnancy. But mifepristone is also used to help with miscarriages, and, the petition notes, miscarriage patients must sign the same form.\par \par \uc2 For years, leading medical organizations have called for removing REMS restrictions on mifepristone, and several lawsuits seeking REMS removal are pending against the F.D.A., including one by attorneys general of 12 other states.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250606el660003h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
