\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Missouri Court Blocks Access to  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Benner \par \uc2 743 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 May 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 19\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The ruling helps undercut a new amendment to the State Constitution that ensures \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The Missouri Supreme Court has temporarily blocked access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state, despite a new amendment to the State Constitution, passed by voters, that ensures such access.\par \par \uc2 The court's ruling on Tuesday lifted an injunction by a circuit judge who had found that some state laws regulating \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access could undermine the new amendment.\par \par \uc2 After the Supreme Court's decision, \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , the state's last \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider, temporarily halted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedures in the state.\par \par \uc2 The State Supreme Court's decision adds to legislative efforts to undercut Amendment 3, a November ballot initiative that guaranteed a ''fundamental right to reproductive freedom,'' including the ability to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It passed with nearly 52 percent of the vote.\par \par \uc2 ''The people spoke through direct democracy, and conservative forces are figuring out how to curtail them,'' said Melissa Murray, a law professor at \uc2 New York University\uc2  who specializes in constitutional law and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Andrew Bailey, the Republican state attorney general, who had appealed the circuit court judge's decision, said, ''This ruling is a win for common sense, for basic medical safety and for the sanctity of human life.''\par \par \uc2 Amendment 3 overturned the state ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that Missouri had enacted in 2022, after the Supreme Court struck down federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization.\par \par \uc2 But dozens of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulations, enacted over the past 20 years, all but banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , making it difficult to implement Amendment 3. Those rules include requirements on wait times before abortions, constraints on where abortions could be performed and licensing regulations that made it easier to shut \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics down.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  state legislators argue that the laws protect patients from medical harm.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Bailey, the state attorney general, said that his Supreme Court win, though temporary, was in line with efforts to make Missouri ''the safest state in the nation for women and families.''\par \par \uc2 The legal battle currently before the Supreme Court started after the passage of Amendment 3, when \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  filed a lawsuit against Missouri's numerous \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, arguing that they undermined the amendment.\par \par \uc2 In December, Judge Jerri Zhang ruled that the state's 2022 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban violated the State Constitution and issued a preliminary injunction against mandatory wait periods.\par \par \uc2 In February, Judge Zhang issued a preliminary injunction against a licensing requirement for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics, calling it ''discriminatory'' because it did ''not treat services provided in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  facilities the same as other types of similarly situated health care, including miscarriage care.'' This injunction lifted a major obstacle to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care and paved the way for \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  to offer the procedure.\par \par \uc2 The Missouri Supreme Court's decision on Tuesday did not weigh in on whether those rules unduly restricted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. It said only that when deciding to halt state laws, Judge Zhang needed to adhere to a higher standard by finding that the evidence in the case supported four elements.\par \par \uc2 Judge Zhang's injunction was based on only one of the four elements -- that \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  was likely to prove that some state laws undermined Amendment 3 and prevail at trial.\par \par \uc2 The State Supreme Court said that she must also show that \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  and its patients will suffer irreparable harm if the state laws at issue are not nullified, that this harm outweighs Missouri's stated interest in protecting consumer safety and that without the injunctions the public interest could be harmed.\par \par \uc2 Michael A. Wolff, a former Missouri Supreme Court justice, said that judges must weigh whether ''the legislature and governor get to override what the people have said they want.''\par \par \uc2 ''Legislators who voted for the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws may be morally offended by this reproductive care,'' Mr. Wolff said. But legally speaking, ''moral offense'' is not enough to undo the voters' decision ''that the interest of the patients are predominate.''\par \par \uc2 This is not the end of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate. The state legislature recently drafted its own constitutional amendment banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  with limited exceptions for medical emergencies, rape and incest. The legislature plans to include the amendment on the 2026 ballot.\par \par \uc2 And while Judge Zhang must determine whether her injunctions meet the required four elements, her decision is only temporary. Mr. Bailey can appeal a new temporary injunction to the Supreme Court. The case goes to trial in January.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250530el5u0003e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
