\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Trump Administration Asks Court to Dismiss  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pill Case, Echoing Biden\b0\par\par\uc2 By Pam Belluck \par \uc2 1,638 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 May 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 21\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The request echoes the position the Biden administration took in the case in January, surprising some observers.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration asked a federal judge on Monday to dismiss a lawsuit that seeks to sharply restrict access to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill mifepristone -- taking the same position as the Biden administration in a closely watched case that has major implications for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 The court filing by the Justice Department is striking, given that President Trump and a number of officials in his administration have forcefully opposed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Mr. Trump often boasts that he appointed three of the Supreme Court justices who voted in 2022 to overturn Roe v. Wade, which had guaranteed the national right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And so far in his second term, his administration has taken steps to curtail programs that support \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The filing is the first time the Trump administration has weighed in on the lawsuit, which seeks to reverse numerous regulatory changes that the Food and Drug Administration made over the past decade that greatly expanded access to mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration's request makes no mention of the merits of the case, which have not yet been considered by the courts. Rather, echoing the argument that the Biden administration made shortly before Mr. Trump took office, the court filing asserts that the case does not meet the legal standard to be heard in the federal district court in which it was filed.\par \par \uc2 The case was filed by the conservative attorneys general of three states -- Missouri, Idaho, and Kansas -- before Judge Matthew J. Kacsmaryk of the U.S. District Court for the Northern District of Texas, a Trump appointee who strongly opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''The states do not dispute that their claims have no connection to the Northern District of Texas,'' the Justice Department lawyers wrote in the filing.\par \par \uc2 ''Regardless of the merits of the states' claims, the states cannot proceed in this court,'' they concluded, adding that the complaint ''should be dismissed or transferred for lack of venue.''\par \par \uc2 Mary Ziegler, a law professor and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law expert at the \uc2 University of California, Davis\uc2 , said that the Trump administration's support of the Biden administration's earlier request to dismiss the case ''is surprising, but I think the best way to read it is that they're just buying time to figure out what to do about mifepristone.''\par \par \uc2 She said the filing ''avoids saying anything on the substance at all,'' which, she suggested, allows the Trump administration to delay telegraphing its views on mifepristone and to control whether and when it takes action to restrict the drug.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump's political calculus on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has changed since his first term. Although Republicans prevailed in the 2024 elections, so did \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, with ballot measures to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access winning in several conservative states, including Missouri. Voters in Kansas, one of the other plaintiffs in the case, endorsed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in 2022, a year when Democrats made strong gains in Congress partly because of a backlash against Republicans over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . During the presidential campaign, Mr. Trump tried to adjust to the changing political winds on the issue, sometimes taking positions that frustrated social conservatives.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Ziegler said Monday's court filing might reflect a desire to be politically cautious on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , possibly until the 2026 midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 ''I think he thinks that doing anything bold on mifepristone could backfire politically,'' she said. ''But he has a lot of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  voters who are not only hoping that he will do something on mifepristone but are still expecting that he will.''\par \par \uc2 ''There hasn't been a merits ruling in this case yet,'' she added, ''so I think the strategy of sort of avoiding the merits and kicking the can down the road is working well for Trump at the moment, but it's sort of unclear whether it's sustainable.''\par \par \uc2 The next step in the case will be for Judge Kacsmaryk to decide whether to dismiss it or allow it to proceed.\par \par \uc2 If the lawsuit succeeded, it could have wide-ranging impact on access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the United States, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills now account for nearly two-thirds of pregnancy terminations.\par \par \uc2 Among the F.D.A. measures the lawsuit seeks to reverse is a provision that removed the requirement that patients visit prescribers in person to obtain mifepristone. Reinstating the in-person requirement would halt the fast-growing practice of prescribing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills through telemedicine and mailing them to patients, including those in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 ''Removing the in-person dispensing protections enabled a 50-state mail-order \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drug economy,'' the attorneys general wrote in their complaint. The lawsuit also seeks to reverse the agency's approval of generic mifepristone, now the most widely used form of the drug; the ability for nurse practitioners and other health providers who are not doctors to prescribe mifepristone; and the ability for retail pharmacies, like CVS and \uc2 Walgreens\uc2 , to dispense the medication.\par \par \uc2 And it asks for new F.D.A. restrictions on mifepristone, including to outlaw the medication for anyone under 18.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit argues that actions by the F.D.A. that expanded access to mifepristone have allowed women to obtain \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills despite state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans or restrictions. Because of this, it says, health systems in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is limited or outlawed are required to treat patients who visit emergency rooms for follow-up care or \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  complications, costing the states money. It also claims that such states have been harmed because the ''loss of fetal life and potential births'' reduces the ''potential population of each state.''\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit also contends that the F.D.A. violated the Comstock Act, a rarely enforced anti-vice law from 1873 that prohibits mailing items ''intended for the prevention of conception or procuring of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .'' A 2022 opinion by the Justice Department said that the law should not be interpreted to criminalize the mailing of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills in most cases. Mr. Trump's Justice Department has not rescinded or changed that opinion.\par \par \uc2 The case was initially filed in November 2022 by a consortium of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors and groups and made its way to the Supreme Court. But in a unanimous ruling in June, the justices threw out the case, saying that the plaintiffs did not have standing to sue because they couldn't show they had been harmed by the F.D.A.'s decisions on mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 A few months later, the three attorneys general revived the lawsuit and filed an amended complaint in the same court in Texas before Judge Kacsmaryk. In the first iteration of the case, Judge Kacsmaryk issued rulings that stridently criticized the F.D.A. and adopted much of the terminology used by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills are prescribed up to 12 weeks into pregnancy in the United States. Women in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans have increasingly sought \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills through telemedicine providers.\par \par \uc2 Currently, 19 states have bans or restrictions stricter than the standard set by Roe v. Wade. In states that support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers have expanded, and a number of states have passed shield laws that protect doctors and other health providers who prescribe and send \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to patients in states with bans or restrictions.\par \par \uc2 The typical medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimen involves mifepristone, which blocks a hormone needed for pregnancy development, followed 24 to 48 hours later by misoprostol, which causes contractions similar to those during a miscarriage.\par \par \uc2 Mifepristone was approved for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  25 years ago. Misoprostol, which has long been widely available for several medical conditions, can terminate a pregnancy on its own, but the lawsuit does not seek any restrictions on misoprostol. Decades of research has found the pills to be safe, and serious complications rare.\par \par \uc2 In January, shortly before Mr. Trump took office, the Biden Justice Department filed a motion to dismiss the lawsuit, citing several reasons, including that the Texas court was the wrong venue. The motion also said that the three states had not shown that they had been concretely harmed by the F.D.A.'s mifepristone regulations and that the states had not taken required steps to first seek the regulatory rollback through F.D.A.'s administrative channels.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration's court filing cites the same reasons. ''The states also contend that F.D.A.'s actions have made it easier for individuals 'to evade state laws,''' the brief said.\par \par \uc2 ''But even assuming that were true,'' it said, ''the mere fact that someone might violate state law'' does not on its own injure a state government in a way that meets the legal standard to sue.\par \par \uc2 Trump administration officials have previously said little about whether they intend to roll back access to mifepristone. Last month, the F.D.A. commissioner, Dr. Martin A. Makary, said during an interview at a journalism conference that he had ''no plans to take action on mifepristone.'' But he also said that ''there is an ongoing set of data that is coming into F.D.A. on mifepristone.''\par \par \uc2 ''So if the data suggests something or tells us that there's a real signal, then I -- we can't promise we're not going to act on that data that we have not yet seen,'' he added.\par \par \uc2 In addition to the F.D.A., the two manufacturers of mifepristone are defendants in the case. \uc2 Danco Laboratories\uc2 , which makes Mifeprex, the branded version of the drug, requested to be added to the case soon after the original filing in 2022. GenBioPro, which makes generic mifepristone, became a party last month after it requested to be added in anticipation that the Trump administration might not fully support the F.D.A.'s actions regarding the medication.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250507el5700033\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
