\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Alabama Can't Prosecute Aiders of Out-of-State Abortions, Judge Rules\b0\par\par\uc2 By Emily Cochrane \par \uc2 976 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 April 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 19\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The state attorney general had raised the possibility of charging doctors with criminal conspiracy for recommending \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care out of state.\par \par \uc2 Alabama cannot prosecute doctors and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  organizations for helping patients travel out of the state to obtain abortions, a federal judge ruled on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Alabama has one of the strictest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in the country, and in 2022 its attorney general, \uc2 Steve Marshall\uc2 , a Republican, raised the possibility of charging doctors with criminal conspiracy for recommending \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care out of state.\par \par \uc2 Multiple clinics and doctors challenged Mr. Marshall's comments in court, accusing him of threatening their First Amendment rights, as well as the constitutional right to travel. The Justice Department under the Biden administration had also weighed in with support for the clinics, arguing that ''threatened criminal prosecutions violate a bedrock principle of American constitutional law.''\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the judge, Myron H. Thompson of the Middle District of Alabama, in Montgomery, ruled that Mr. Marshall would be violating both the First Amendment and the right to travel if he sought prosecution.\par \par \uc2 ''It is one thing for Alabama to outlaw by statute what happens in its own backyard,'' Judge Thompson, who was named to the court by President Jimmy Carter, wrote in his 131-page opinion.\par \par \uc2 ''It is another thing,'' he added, ''for the state to enforce its values and laws, as chosen by the attorney general, outside its boundaries by punishing its citizens and others who help individuals travel to another state to engage in conduct that is lawful there but the attorney general finds to be contrary to Alabama's values and laws.''\par \par \uc2 Judge Thompson described a hypothetical scenario in which a bachelor party from Alabama could be prosecuted for casino-style gambling in Las Vegas, which is illegal in Alabama.\par \par \uc2 ''As the adage goes, be careful what you pray for,'' he wrote.\par \par \uc2 Travel to other states to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, has significantly increased since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade. More than 171,000 patients traveled for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2023, compared with 73,100 in 2019, according to the research organization \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Marshall repeatedly defended his position in court, arguing that he retained the ability to prosecute a conspiracy that took place in Alabama and that the legality of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws in other states did not matter. (He does not appear to have charged anyone in such a case.)\par \par \uc2 ''The right to travel, to the extent that it is even implicated, does not grant plaintiffs the right to carry out a criminal conspiracy simply because they propose to do so by purchasing bus passes or driving cars,'' Mr. Marshall wrote in one filing.\par \par \uc2 Republican-led states, like Alabama, generally have the most restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws in the country. Some of those states are now taking legal steps to stop out-of-state efforts to help residents obtain abortions.\par \par \uc2 Louisiana, which passed a law last year designating \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills as dangerous controlled substances, has charged both a Louisiana mother and a New York doctor with violating the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. (New York has declined to extradite the doctor.)\par \par \uc2 And this month, a New York county clerk blocked Texas from filing legal action against the same doctor. New York has an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  shield law that prevents penalties against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers who use telemedicine to send medications to other states.\par \par \uc2 The Alabama ruling could be appealed, as the judicial system continues to grapple with the fallout from Roe. In June, the Supreme Court temporarily allowed for emergency abortions in Idaho, though it did not weigh in directly on the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 Alabama, where voters approved a constitutional amendment in 2018 aimed at protecting the rights of unborn children, has been at the center of the debate over reproductive medicine and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. It has one of the strictest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in the nation, with an exception only if the life of a pregnant woman is at risk. It also allows for doctors to be charged with felonies that carry sentences of up to 99 years in prison.\par \par \uc2 And its anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  amendment was at the heart of a State Supreme Court decision last year that found that embryos could be considered children, a decision that briefly paralyzed fertility treatments in the state and thrust the issue of in vitro fertilization into the national spotlight.\par \par \uc2 The clinics that first challenged Mr. Marshall's comments, in 2023, included the Yellowhammer Fund, an organization founded in Tuscaloosa that helps fund and support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the Deep South, and the West Alabama Women's Center in Tuscaloosa, now known as WAWC Healthcare. The plaintiffs also included Dr. Yashica Robinson, an obstetrician-gynecologist in Huntsville.\par \par \uc2 In court filings, they said they either had stopped operating an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund or had begun declining to answer questions about how patients could seek care out of state. Collectively, the plaintiffs still receive several calls a week asking for help; the court ruling on Monday put the figure at as many as 95 a week.\par \par \uc2 ''Every day was agonizing,'' said Kelsea McLain, the health care access director for the Yellowhammer Fund. The ruling, she said, brought ''just an overwhelming sense of relief.''\par \par \uc2 ''We are free to do exactly what we feel called to do, in ways that we are experts in,'' she added. ''People won't be alone.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Marshall's office said it was ''reviewing the decision to determine the state's options.''\par \par \uc2 Notably, in a 2022 opinion concurring with the decision to overturn Roe, Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  wrote that he did not believe a state could constitutionally bar a resident from traveling for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Judge Thompson noted this in his ruling on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Abbie VanSickle contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Abbie VanSickle contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250402el4200057\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
