\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 New York County Blocks Action Against Doctor Over  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pills\b0\par\par\uc2 By Pam Belluck \par \uc2 1,083 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 March 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 20\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The showdown catapults the interstate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars to a new level.\par \par \uc2 A New York county clerk on Thursday blocked Texas from filing a legal action against a New York doctor for prescribing and sending \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to a Texas woman.\par \par \uc2 The unprecedented move catapults the interstate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars to a new level, setting the stage for a high-stakes legal battle between states that ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and states that support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The dispute is widely expected to reach the Supreme Court, pitting Texas, which has a near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, against New York, which has a shield law that is intended to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers who send medications to patients in other states.\par \par \uc2 New York is one of eight states that have enacted ''telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  shield laws'' after the Supreme Court overturned the national right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2022. The laws prevent officials from extraditing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers to other states or from responding to subpoenas and other legal actions -- a stark departure from typical interstate practices of cooperating in such cases.\par \par \uc2 The action by the New York county clerk is the first time that an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  shield law has been used to refuse the enforcement of an out-of-state judgment.\par \par \uc2 This case involves Dr. Margaret Daley Carpenter of New Paltz, N.Y., who works with telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  organizations to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to patients across the country. In December, the Texas attorney general, \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , sued Dr. Carpenter, who is not licensed in Texas, accusing her of sending \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to a Texas woman, in violation of the state's ban.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Carpenter and her lawyers did not respond to the lawsuit and did not show up for a court hearing last month in Texas. Judge Bryan Gantt of Collin County District Court issued a default judgment, ordering Dr. Carpenter to pay a penalty of $113,000 and to stop sending \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication to Texas.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, citing New York's shield law, the acting clerk of Ulster County in Kingston, N.Y., Taylor Bruck, said he would not grant Texas' motion seeking to enforce the Collin County order. He also refused to allow Texas to file a summons that sought to force Dr. Carpenter to pay the penalty and comply with the Texas ruling.\par \par \uc2 ''In accordance with the New York State Shield Law, I have refused this filing and will refuse any similar filings that may come to our office,'' Mr. Bruck said in a statement. ''Since this decision is likely to result in further litigation, I must refrain from discussing specific details about the situation.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Paxton, the Texas attorney general, vowed to press on. ''I am outraged that New York would refuse to allow Texas to pursue enforcement of a civil judgment against a radical abortionist illegally peddling dangerous drugs across state lines,'' he said in a statement. ''New York is shredding the Constitution to hide lawbreakers from justice, and it must end. I will not stop my efforts to enforce Texas's pro-life laws that protect our unborn children and mothers.''\par \par \uc2 Legal experts said that a likely next step would be for Texas to file a challenge to the shield law in a state or federal court in New York.\par \par \uc2 New York's attorney general, Letitia James, had previously sent guidance to courts and officials throughout the state, directing them to follow the shield law and indicating how they could comply and which specific actions were prohibited.\par \par \uc2 ''I commend the Ulster County Clerk for doing what is right,'' Ms. James said in a statement. ''New York's shield law was created to protect patients and providers from out-of-state anti-choice attacks, and we will not allow anyone to undermine health care providers' ability to deliver necessary care to their patients. My office will always defend New York's medical professionals and the people they serve.''\par \par \uc2 Texas was the first state with an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban to initiate legal action against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in states with shield laws. In January, the first criminal charges against a shield-law \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider were filed in a second state, Louisiana. In that case, a state grand jury issued a criminal indictment, also against Dr. Carpenter, accusing her of violating Louisiana's near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban by sending pills to that state.\par \par \uc2 Last month, Louisiana officials issued an extradition order for Dr. Carpenter, which was immediately rebuffed by New York's governor, \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''I will not be signing an extradition order that came from the governor of Louisiana -- not now, not ever,'' Ms. Hochul said then.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Carpenter and her lawyers have not commented about either the Texas or Louisiana case. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Coalition for Telemedicine, an organization Dr. Carpenter co-founded, has issued statements in response to the cases. ''Shield laws are essential in safeguarding and enabling \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care regardless of a patient's ZIP code or ability to pay,'' the coalition has said. ''They are fundamental to ensuring everyone can access \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care as a human right.''\par \par \uc2 Telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  shield laws have become a key strategy for supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Under these laws, which have been in use since summer 2023, health care providers in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal have been sending more than 10,000 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills per month to patients in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans or restrictions.\par \par \uc2 The Texas lawsuit accuses Dr. Carpenter of providing a 20-year-old woman with the two medications used in a standard \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimen, mifepristone and misoprostol. Typically used up through 12 weeks into pregnancy, mifepristone blocks a hormone needed for pregnancies to develop, and misoprostol, taken 24 to 48 hours later, causes contractions similar to a miscarriage.\par \par \uc2 According to a complaint filed by the Texas attorney general's office, the woman, who had been nine weeks pregnant, asked the ''biological father of her unborn child'' to take her to the emergency room in July ''because of hemorrhage or severe bleeding.'' The man ''suspected that the biological mother had in fact done something to contribute to the miscarriage,'' the suit said, and he went back to their home in Collin County, where he ''discovered the two above-referenced medications from Carpenter.''\par \par \uc2 In the Collin County court hearing last month, Ernest C. Garcia, chief of the administrative law division in the attorney general's office, said that the man ''then filed a complaint with the Texas Attorney General's Office.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250328el3s0003j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
