\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Primary  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pill Maker Moves to Enter Court Battle Over F.D.A. Rules\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lisa Lerer \par \uc2 1,020 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 February 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GenBioPro, a leading manufacturer of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication, is preparing to go to battle with the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 The country's largest manufacturer of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills is wading into the first major legal battle over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  of President Trump's second term.\par \par \uc2 The company, GenBioPro, on Tuesday asked a Texas court to add it to the list of defendants in a lawsuit filed in October by three Republican state attorneys general. The move was a significant offensive action on an issue seen as a vanguard in the fight over access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit was filed by the state attorneys general from Missouri, Idaho and Kansas, and asks a federal court to reverse a series of Food and Drug Administration regulations that have greatly expanded access to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 Under the Biden administration, the Justice Department defended the agency's rules and F.D.A.'s approval of the medication 25 years ago. But many \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates anticipate that the Trump administration will decline to defend the agency, effectively siding with the state attorneys general and using the case to limit access.\par \par \uc2 If the judge grants GenBioPro's request, the maneuver will allow the company to lead the defense of mifepristone. The company is being represented by Democracy Forward, a legal nonprofit that has filed more than a dozen lawsuits and won multiple court orders against the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 ''The foundation of these extreme politicians' arguments are purely political, rather than based in scientific evidence,'' said Skye Perryman, the president and chief executive of Democracy Forward. ''The threat this case brings to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access nationwide cannot be understated.''\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills are prescribed up to 12 weeks into pregnancy and are used in nearly two-thirds of abortions in the United States. In states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is criminalized or heavily restricted, mail-order medication has become the central way to receive the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Since the Supreme Court eliminated the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the 2022 case of Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, health care providers in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal say they have sent more than 10,000 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills per month to patients in those states.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents have asked the administration to roll back rules that allow the pills to be prescribed by telehealth appointments and sent through the mail. They argue that in-person medical visits are necessary to safeguard patients. Such efforts are part of their mission to end all abortions. More than 100 scientific studies, conducted over decades, have found the pills to be safe and to rarely cause serious complications.\par \par \uc2 GenBioPro, which manufactures only \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication, controls roughly two-thirds of the market for the drug. The only other mifepristone manufacturer in the country, Danco Laboratories, is already named as a defendant in the suit.\par \par \uc2 ''We remain concerned about extremists and special interests' attempts to undermine the \uc2 U.S. Food and Drug Administration\uc2 's regulatory authority,'' said Evan Masingill, the chief executive of GenBioPro. ''All people have a right to access safe, affordable, evidence-based health care, and GenBioPro remains committed to using all legal and regulatory tools to protect mifepristone for millions of patients and providers across the country.''\par \par \uc2 Whether Mr. Trump will take action to restrict the pills remains unclear. During his presidential campaign last year, he promised to leave \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulation to individual states. After winning the White House, Mr. Trump said it was ''unlikely, very unlikely'' that he would authorize additional steps to limit access to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill. But he refused to rule out the idea completely, telling an interviewer that people ''feel strongly both ways.'' He faces intense public and private pressure from the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  wing of his party to criminalize the medication.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2 , Republican of Missouri, said he specifically asked Mr. Trump in a private conversation to reinstate rules that require patients to pick up \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication in person. The move would severely curtail access for women in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 ''Those requirements were never controversial until Joe Biden decided that this was a good way to get around Dobbs,'' said Mr. Hawley in an interview last month. ''It's an easy call to put the in-person dispensing requirement back in place, and I hope that the administration will do that.''\par \par \uc2 The legal fight over the pill began just months after the Dobbs ruling, when a coalition of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights organizations and doctors asked a Texas court to invalidate the F.D.A.'s approval of the pill in 2000.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court eventually rejected their argument, saying that the groups lacked legal standing to sue because they could not show evidence of being harmed by the pills.\par \par \uc2 The latest lawsuit is a revised and expanded version of that case. It directly challenges GenBioPro's approval for generic mifepristone, which was granted in 2019, and takes aim at a wider range of rules intended to expand access to the pills.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit asks the court to outlaw the medication for anyone under 18, reinstate the in-person appointment requirement, mandate that only doctors can prescribe the pills and limit the ability of retail pharmacies, like CVS and \uc2 Walgreens\uc2 , to dispense the medication.\par \par \uc2 The complaint also criticizes shield laws, which have been passed in eight states, that protect doctors and other health providers who prescribe and send \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to patients in states with bans or restrictions. Lawsuits brought by conservatives in Louisiana and Texas are challenging the protections offered by such laws.\par \par \uc2 Rather than file the case in one of their home states, the state attorneys general brought it before Judge Matthew J. Kacsmaryk, of the U.S. District Court for the Northern District of Texas, a Trump appointee who opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and who heard the original lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 In April 2023, Judge Kacsmaryk ruled in favor of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups, issuing a sweeping preliminary injunction invalidating the F.D.A.'s initial approval of mifepristone. His ruling was eventually rejected by the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250226el2q0004i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
