\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Missouri Clinics Resume Abortions After a Referendum and Lawsuits\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Zernike \par \uc2 866 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 February 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 22\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents had tried to block, or severely limit, the procedure, against the will of voters who in November enshrined \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the state constitution.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in the staunchly Republican state of Missouri this week resumed procedures for the first time in years, despite a continued push by conservative state leaders to block a constitutional amendment enshrining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights that voters approved in November.\par \par \uc2 It was a remarkable moment after an extended fight. Missouri was the first state to enact an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade in 2022. Then in 2024, it became the first state with a near-total ban to approve a citizen-sponsored \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights amendment.\par \par \uc2 On the day after voters approved the constitutional amendment, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups sued to overturn the ban as well as a host of other restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that preceded the ban. \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , the only provider of abortions outside of hospitals in the state, resumed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedures after a judge on Friday granted a temporary injunction that blocked state licensing requirements imposed on clinics. The clinics had said that the requirements made it impossible to operate.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  still will not provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills until the state approves a required plan for reporting any complications faced by women who use them. And Republican legislators are still pressing for a raft of bills that would restrict or reverse the amendment passed in November.\par \par \uc2 The struggle to provide abortions three months after the ballot measure passed highlights the difficulties that clinics face despite popular support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights among voters.\par \par \uc2 Still, Emily Wales, the president of Planned Parenthood Great Plains, said that relative to other states with bans, Missouri was moving at ''breakneck speed.''\par \par \uc2 ''It feels like a long time for Missourians,'' she said, ''and every single day that there are people in the state who have to leave for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, their rights are being violated.''\par \par \uc2 ''At the same time,'' she added, ''constitutional change is huge. We didn't know we could even take on this fight, or that we'd be successful, or that we'd get beyond all the barriers to be on the ballot a year ago.''\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Bell, a spokeswoman for Missouri Stands with Women, one of the groups that opposed the ballot measure in November, criticized the judge for allowing ''unlicensed and unregulated \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics prioritizing profits over safety.''\par \par \uc2 ''No woman should suffer, and no innocent life should be taken, in the name of an industry that refuses to be held accountable,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 The amendment, approved by nearly 52 percent of Missouri voters, established a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until viability, the point in pregnancy when doctors generally believe a fetus can survive outside the uterus without extraordinary medical interventions.\par \par \uc2 Judge Jerri Zhang of Jackson County Circuit Court, which includes Kansas City, issued an earlier injunction in December, two weeks after the amendment took effect, that invalidated the near-total ban and blocked some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions. Those restrictions included a requirement that patients wait 72 hours between an initial consultation with the provider and receiving an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and another requirement that patients see the same doctor for both appointments.\par \par \uc2 That ruling allowed hospitals to offer abortions, including for miscarriage care, without the threat of criminal penalties. But \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  postponed plans to resume \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services because the judge declined to overturn the licensing requirements.\par \par \uc2 To get a license, clinics had to abide by a list of rules that included minimum sizes for hallways, rooms and doors, extensive reporting requirements and mandatory pelvic exams for patients seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, contradicting standard medical practice.\par \par \uc2 The requirements were so onerous that most clinics stopped providing abortions long before the Supreme Court overturned the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; state reports show that there were 167 abortions in Missouri in 2020, down from about 5,000 a decade before, even as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained legal.\par \par \uc2 In her ruling late Friday, Judge Zhang said the licensing requirement was discriminatory, ''because it does not treat services provided in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  facilities the same as other types of similarly situated health care, including miscarriage care.''\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  had noted that Missouri did not require the same licensing for clinics that offer vasectomies, cataract removals, liposuction and other surgeries, including those that involve general anesthesia. Nor did it have the same requirements for private physicians who do miscarriage management using the same medicines and procedures that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics use.\par \par \uc2 Attorney General Andrew Bailey of Missouri, a Republican who opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, is expected to appeal the judge's ruling; a spokeswoman did not return requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Republicans who control the legislature have filed more than two dozen bills proposing limits to the amendment voters approved in November, including some that would ask voters to approve a contradictory amendment banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in all or most cases.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Action Missouri, which supported the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights amendment in November, announced that it would expand its clinic escorts program. A group that opposed the ballot measure, Coalition Life, said it would resume protests and sidewalk counseling outside the \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  clinic in St. Louis.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250221el2l0002z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
