\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Infant Mortality and Births Rose After  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Bans, Studies Find\b0\par\par\uc2 By Pam Belluck \par \uc2 1,325 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 February 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 19\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The findings showed the highest mortality occurred among infants who were Black, lived in Southern states or had fetal birth defects.\par \par \uc2 Infant mortality increased along with births in most states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in the first 18 months after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, according to new research.\par \par \uc2 The findings, in two studies published Thursday in the journal JAMA, also suggest that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans can have the most significant effects on people who are struggling economically or who are in other types of challenging circumstances, health policy experts said.\par \par \uc2 ''The groups that are most likely to have children as a result of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans are also individuals who are most likely, for a number of different reasons, to have higher rates of infant mortality,'' said Alyssa Bilinski, a professor of health policy at Brown University, who was not involved in the research.\par \par \uc2 Overall, infant mortality was 6 percent higher than expected in states that implemented \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, said Alison Gemmill, one of the researchers, who is a demographer and perinatal epidemiologist in the department of population, family and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the \uc2 Johns Hopkins Bloomberg School of Public Health\uc2 . That number reflected increases in nine states, decreases in four and no change in one.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Gemmill said that among non-Hispanic Black infants, mortality was 11 percent higher after \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans were implemented than would have been expected. Also, there were more babies born with congenital birth defects, situations in which women have been able to terminate their pregnancies if not for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 Overall, the researchers found that in the states that implemented near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans or bans after six weeks' gestation during that period, there were 478 more deaths of babies in their first year of life after the bans were implemented than would have been expected based on previous years' data.\par \par \uc2 Birthrate increases were higher among communities with socioeconomic disadvantages and in states that have the worst maternal and child health outcomes.\par \par \uc2 ''What happens when you ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is that you create enormous inequality in access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' said Caitlin Myers of \uc2 Middlebury College\uc2 , who studies similar \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  data but was not involved in the new research.\par \par \uc2 The studies evaluated data from birth and death certificates and census records for all 50 states from January 2012 through December 2023. That time frame allowed researchers to compare trends in births and infant mortality in the years before the Supreme Court overturned the national right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in June 2022 with data in the 18 months afterward.\par \par \uc2 At the time, 14 states had implemented near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans or bans after six weeks' gestation during that period. Now 16 have.\par \par \uc2 While national data has shown that, because of factors like telemedicine and out-of-state travel, overall \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates have actually increased since the Supreme Court's ruling, that does not mean that everyone who needed or sought an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could obtain one, Dr. Myers said.\par \par \uc2 She said the research showed that two dynamics were behind the increase in infant mortality. One aspect is that when women are not allowed to end pregnancies of fetuses with congenital anomalies, the babies often die within days or weeks after birth.\par \par \uc2 The other aspect is that women who cannot obtain abortions by traveling to other states or by ordering pills by mail are ''more likely to be poor, more likely to be women of color, and those populations have higher rates of maternal morbidity and mortality, infant complications, infant mortality,'' Dr. Myers said.\par \par \uc2 Much of the overall increase was driven by data from Texas, said Suzanne Bell, a co-author of the studies and a professor in the same department at Johns Hopkins as Dr. Gemmill. Dr. Bell said all but 94 of the additional 478 infant deaths were in Texas, which has a much larger population than any of the other states with bans.\par \par \uc2 Infant mortality in Texas was 9.4 percent higher after \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans were implemented than would have been expected, the research found. In the eight other states with bans that showed increases, that rate ranged from a 1.3 percent increase in Mississippi to an 8.6 percent increase in Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 The researchers attributed the dominant influence of Texas on the data partly to the fact that in September 2021, about nine months before the Supreme Court's Dobbs decision, Texas implemented a strict ban on abortions after six weeks of pregnancy. Another factor, Dr. Bell said, was that before that time, a relatively high proportion of Texas women seeking abortions were able to obtain them from health care providers there, but after the bans, clinics and other \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services closed, forcing women to travel long distances across that sprawling state.\par \par \uc2 In many of the other states, Dr. Bell said, there were already very few \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers before the bans, so women ''were already traveling out of state or were already unable to obtain \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Five states with bans did not show higher infant mortality than expected. In Louisiana, the rate did not change. In Idaho, Missouri, West Virginia and Wisconsin, the rate decreased. The researchers said that was most likely because neighboring states, including Illinois, Washington State and Maryland, were providing expanded access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In addition, they said, the demographics and relatively low socioeconomic status of residents in most of the Southern states contributed to higher infant mortality and higher birthrates after \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans were imposed.\par \par \uc2 ''There are just very longstanding disparities in these outcomes that are shaped by state policies,'' Dr. Gemmill said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents said they had a different interpretation of the data.\par \par \uc2 ''All of these 'excess' children who were born would have been killed in induced abortions,'' said Dr. Donna Harrison, who is director of research at American Association of Pro-Life Obstetrician and Gynecologists. ''This means that anyone lamenting the results of this study isn't really concerned that these babies died; rather, they wish they would have been killed earlier: in the womb.''\par \par \uc2 The analysis of birth data found that in the states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, the rate of births per 1,000 women of reproductive age increased by 1.7 percent more than would be expected from previous years' data.\par \par \uc2 ''It might seem like a 1.7 percent change in the fertility rate isn't a big deal, but it's actually a very big deal,'' Dr. Gemmill said. She said that demographers considered such an increase very significant and noted that it was higher than the 1.4 percent increase in birthrates related to the Covid pandemic.\par \par \uc2 In states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, that increase translated to 22,180 more births than expected, the researchers said.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Bilinski, who wrote an editorial accompanying the studies, said that the findings presented an opportunity to galvanize efforts to improve support systems and funding for pregnant women and infants -- regardless of one's views on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''These papers are not going to resolve disagreements about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in this country,'' she said. ''People are going to look at these papers, and particularly the results about birthrates, and I think have very different reactions.''\par \par \uc2 But nobody is in favor of infants dying. ''We should want to prevent infant mortality, and in many cases infant mortality is preventable,'' she said. She added, ''If we are in a world where more people who perhaps didn't plan to and didn't feel prepared to become parents are becoming parents, we should think about what it means to be supporting those families in a real and tangible way.''\par \par \uc2 Dr. Bilinski said the study results underscored the need for policies and programs like Medicaid, the child tax credit, parental leave and affordable child care.\par \par \uc2 ''I would hope that as a country, looking at these results,'' she said, ''we can all kind of agree that those children and families should have an opportunity to thrive.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250214el2e0003e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\