\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Texas Judge Tells New York Doctor to Stop Sending  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pills\b0\par\par\uc2 By Pam Belluck and Mary Beth Gahan \par \uc2 1,292 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 February 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 19\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 It is the first ruling in a case challenging ''shield laws'' intended to protect doctors in states that support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights who send \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to states with bans.\par \par \uc2 In a case that could have major implications for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the United States, a Texas judge on Thursday ordered a New York doctor to stop prescribing and sending \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to patients in Texas and to pay a penalty of more than $100,000 for providing the medication to one woman.\par \par \uc2 The case is widely expected to reach the Supreme Court and become a pivotal test in the escalating battle between states that ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and states that support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. It essentially pits Texas, which has a near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, against New York, which has a ''telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  shield law'' intended to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers who send medications to patients in other states.\par \par \uc2 These shield laws have become a key \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights strategy since the Supreme Court overturned the national right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2022. The laws, enacted in eight states so far, stipulate that officials and agencies will not cooperate with civil suits, prosecutions or other legal actions filed against health care providers who prescribe and send \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication to patients in other states.\par \par \uc2 Such laws represent a stark departure from typical interstate practices of extraditing, honoring subpoenas and sharing information. Under telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  shield laws, which have been in use since summer 2023, health care providers in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal have been sending more than 10,000 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills per month to patients in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans or restrictions.\par \par \uc2 The Texas lawsuit was filed in December by the Texas attorney general, \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , against Dr. Margaret Daley Carpenter of New Paltz, N.Y., who works with telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  organizations to provide pills to patients across the country. The suit alleges that Dr. Carpenter, who is not licensed in Texas, supplied \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to a woman in Texas.\par \par \uc2 The order signed on Thursday by Judge Bryan Gantt of Collin County District Court said that Dr. Carpenter ''is permanently enjoined from prescribing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -inducing drugs to Texas residents.'' Violating an injunction can result in a contempt order from a judge, which could carry additional financial penalties or a jail sentence. The judge also ordered a $100,000 fine and about $13,000 in attorneys' fees and court costs plus interest.\par \par \uc2 With New York's shield law prohibiting cooperation with out-of-state legal actions, Dr. Carpenter and her lawyers did not respond to the Texas suit or appear in court on Wednesday for a hearing before the judge.\par \par \uc2 The 40-minute session in the court north of Dallas was notably quiet and sedate for an issue of such controversy and national significance.\par \par \uc2 Two lawyers for the attorney general's office asked the judge to issue a default judgment in their favor, essentially a ruling against a defendant who has not shown up or provided any response.\par \par \uc2 The attorney general's lawyers argued in court filings that because Dr. Carpenter did not respond within a certain period of time, Texas law deems that ''defendant by her non-answer has admitted all of the plaintiffs' allegations of fact establishing liability.''\par \par \uc2 The defense table was empty. About 30 minutes into the hearing, Judge Gantt said, ''I noticed she is not here.'' He asked the attorney general's lawyers if they had heard from Dr. Carpenter that morning.\par \par \uc2 When they said no, the judge asked the bailiff to ''call the hall'' and announce Dr. Carpenter's name in the corridor outside the courtroom. Less than a minute later, the bailiff returned and said, ''Your honor, I called Margaret Daley Carpenter three times with no response.''\par \par \uc2 Texas was the first state with an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban to initiate legal action against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in states with shield laws. But other states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans are expected to follow suit.\par \par \uc2 In January, the first criminal charges against a shield-law \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider were filed. In that case, a state grand jury in Louisiana issued a criminal indictment, also against Dr. Carpenter, accusing her of violating Louisiana's near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban by sending pills to that state.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, Louisiana's governor, \uc2 Jeff Landry\uc2 , said he had signed a warrant seeking to extradite Dr. Carpenter to his state to stand trial. New York's governor, \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , responded by citing the state's shield law and saying, ''I will not be signing an extradition order that came from the governor of Louisiana, not now, not ever.''\par \par \uc2 The Texas and Louisiana cases are each expected to lead to court battles with the state of New York.\par \par \uc2 New York's refusal could lead Louisiana to ask the federal courts to order extradition, experts said. The potential outcome is unclear, but Mary Ziegler, a law professor and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  expert at the \uc2 University of California, Davis\uc2 , said there was legal precedent for extradition not being required for defendants who were not in the state where the alleged crime was committed and did not flee from that state.\par \par \uc2 In the civil case, Texas is considered likely to file a petition in a state court in New York to try to collect the financial penalty. If New York were to cite its shield law to argue against the Texas penalty, as expected, the case could transform into a battle in federal court or the Supreme Court over whether the shield law is constitutional in allowing one state to refuse to cooperate with another state's legal actions.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Carpenter has not been reachable for comment about either the Texas or Louisiana case. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Coalition for Telemedicine, an organization she co-founded, has issued statements in response to the cases. ''Shield laws are essential in safeguarding and enabling \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care regardless of a patient's ZIP code or ability to pay,'' the coalition has said. ''They are fundamental to ensuring everyone can access \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care as a human right.''\par \par \uc2 The Texas lawsuit accuses Dr. Carpenter of providing a 20-year-old woman with the two medications used in a standard \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimen, mifepristone and misoprostol. Typically used up through 12 weeks into pregnancy, mifepristone blocks a hormone needed for pregnancies to develop, and misoprostol, taken 24 to 48 hours later, causes contractions similar to a miscarriage.\par \par \uc2 According to a complaint filed by the Texas attorney general's office, the woman, who had been nine weeks pregnant, asked the ''biological father of her unborn child'' to take her to the emergency room in July ''because of hemorrhage or severe bleeding.'' In court on Wednesday, Ernest C. Garcia, chief of the administrative law division in the attorney general's office, said that at the hospital, the woman's partner ''ended up finding out that she had been pregnant'' and that ''he then started to suspect that maybe she had not been truthful about it.''\par \par \uc2 When the man returned to the house, he found the medications and realized that they had been taken to induce an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Mr. Garcia said, adding ''that individual then filed a complaint with the Texas Attorney General's Office.''\par \par \uc2 The Texas case is an example of an increasing pattern in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans: men reporting to the authorities that their female partners had abortions. There have been other such cases in Texas, and John Seago, the president of Texas Right to Life, said in an interview that, in the coming weeks, several men plan to file suits for wrongful death against doctors, organizations or people who assisted in arranging abortions for the men's female partners.\par \par \uc2 Emily Cochrane contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Emily Cochrane contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250214el2e0003f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
