\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECT\par \b \uc2 Trump Signs Order to Fight 'Anti-Christian Bias'\b0\par\par\uc2 By Erica L. Green \par \uc2 609 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 February 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 The New York Times on the Web\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The president convened a task force to ''prosecute anti-Christian violence and vandalism.'' Critics said he'd turned religious freedom on its head by singling out one faith.\par \par \uc2 President Trump signed an executive order on Thursday aimed at eradicating ''anti-Christian bias'' in the federal government by having agencies review policies and practices that he says have tried to squelch religious activities and activism.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump, who announced the order at the National Prayer Breakfast, appointed his new attorney general, Pam Bondi, to lead a task force at the Justice Department to spearhead the effort. Mr. Trump said the task force would ''fully prosecute anti-Christian violence and vandalism in our society'' and ''move heaven and earth to defend the rights of Christians and religious believers nationwide.''\par \par \uc2 ''You've never had that before, but this is a very powerful document I'm signing,'' he said. ''You got it now -- first time you've had it. If we don't have religious liberty, then we don't have a free country. We probably don't even have a country.''\par \par \uc2 The executive order is meant to reverse what it calls an ''egregious pattern of targeting peaceful Christians, while ignoring violent, anti-Christian offenses'' under President \uc2 Joseph R. Biden Jr.\uc2  It cited the convictions of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  demonstrators for blocking access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics.\par \par \uc2 Critics said the order and the task force turned the nation's traditional understanding of religious freedom, established by the First Amendment and reinforced by decades of laws and Supreme Court decisions, on its head by singling out a particular religion.\par \par \uc2 ''Rather than protecting religious beliefs, this task force will misuse religious freedom to justify bigotry, discrimination and the subversion of our civil rights laws,'' Rachel Laser, the president of \uc2 Americans United for Separation of Church and State\uc2 , said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 She added, ''If Trump really cared about religious freedom and ending religious persecution, he'd be addressing antisemitism in his inner circle, anti-Muslim bigotry, hate crimes against people of color and other religious minorities.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump said he would also convene a new presidential commission on religious liberty, which he called ''a very big deal.'' He also plans to create a new faith office in the White House led by the Rev. Paula White, a televangelist and longtime faith adviser to Mr. Trump whose populist interpretation of the Gospel has proved divisive among Christians.\par \par \uc2 During his remarks Thursday, Mr. Trump condemned the conviction of an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activist who attended the prayer breakfast, Paulette Harlow. She was sentenced to prison in May for blocking access to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic. Mr. Trump pardoned Ms. Harlow and other anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  demonstrators days after taking office.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump accused the Justice Department, the I.R.S. and F.B.I. of being key perpetrators of religious persecution, in line with long-running claims by conservatives that the Biden administration had used those agencies to carry out an agenda biased against conservative Christians.\par \par \uc2 Congressional Republicans have complained loudly in particular about a 2023 memo issued by the F.B.I.'s Richmond office that said far-right extremists might be attracted to Catholic churches or groups. That led to a contentious hearing with the F.B.I. director.\par \par \uc2 The F.B.I. has worked to develop sources at churches, universities and mosques for decades, but Republicans have argued that the Richmond memo showed it was targeting Catholics.\par \par \uc2 F.B.I. officials withdrew the memo, and an internal investigation found no evidence of ''malicious intent'' behind it.\par \par \uc2 Devlin Barrett contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Devlin Barrett contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250207el2700057\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
