\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 New York Strengthens Protection for Doctors Who Send  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pills\b0\par\par\uc2 By Benjamin Oreskes \par \uc2 596 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 February 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 20\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  signed a bill meant to protect medical practitioners in New York who prescribe and send \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills out of state.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  of New York signed a bill on Monday intended to give the state's health care providers an extra layer of protection to shield them from prosecution in states that ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The newly signed law comes days after a New York doctor was indicted in Louisiana for prescribing and sending \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to someone in the state. The charges represented an escalation in the fractious battle between mostly Republican-led states that ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Democratic-led states seeking to protect or expand \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 The law, which takes effect immediately, will allow health-care practitioners to avoid putting their names on prescriptions for medications used in abortions, and instead use the names of their medical practices.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hochul, a Democrat, said the goal was to better conceal the identity of providers in hopes of protecting them from criminal, civil or other legal action that anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  states try to take against them.\par \par \uc2 ''Other states want to target, harass, scare, intimidate doctors and patients. That may be OK in a place like Louisiana -- maybe Indiana,'' Ms. Hochul said at a news conference on Monday. ''But those are not our values here in the state of New York.''\par \par \uc2 The legislation signed on Monday augments the state's telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  shield law, under which New York authorities are barred from cooperating with a prosecution or other action taken by another state against a New York \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider.\par \par \uc2 New York is one of eight states to have adopted such laws. Sending the pills across state lines has become a key way to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access to women in states with bans.\par \par \uc2 Since the Supreme Court overturned the national right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2022, a dozen states have enacted near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, and others have imposed strict limits on when during a pregnancy an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is allowed.\par \par \uc2 In the Louisiana case, Dr. Margaret Carpenter of New Paltz, N.Y., was charged last week, along with her medical practice, for ''criminal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  by means of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -inducing drugs.''\par \par \uc2 Dr. Carpenter, who could not be reached for comment on Monday, was also sued in December by the attorney general of Texas and accused of providing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to a 20-year-old woman in that state.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hochul said Monday that she would be looking for ways to further strengthen New York's law, including ensuring that pharmacies follow the new labeling rules.\par \par \uc2 State Senator Michelle Hinchey, a New York Democrat who is a friend of Dr. Carpenter's, said the doctor was ''doing the hard work right now, standing up for the rights of people in other states that are losing their autonomy.''\par \par \uc2 Louisiana law enforcement officials filed charges against the mother of the teenager who took the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills as well. The officials also said the teenager was coerced into taking the medication.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hochul said she was not sure if that was true, and questioned if it was a ''Republican distraction.''\par \par \uc2 ''Doctors have been stopped, some receiving death threats, and in deeply conservative states they weaponized the courts against providers,'' Ms. Hochul said. ''And now we know they've tried to strip New York doctors of their medical licenses, sue them, throw them in jail and even charge them with murder.''\par \par \uc2 Pam Belluck and Emily Cochrane contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Pam Belluck and Emily Cochrane contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250204el240004j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
