\par\par \uc2 MICHELLE GOLDBERG\par \uc2 Sunday Review Desk; SECTSR\par \b \uc2 Under Trump, a Wink and a Nod at Anti- \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Violence\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michelle Goldberg \par \uc2 976 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 February 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Three days after getting out of federal prison for his role in forcibly shutting down a \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic in Washington, D.C., Herb Geraghty, one of 23 anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists pardoned by President Trump last week, was eager to return to protesting. ''I'm planning to run by an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic as soon as I get the opportunity and participate in some peaceful sidewalk advocacy,'' he told me on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Geraghty, 29, hoped that the new administration would, by restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , eventually make the sort of radical action that led to his conviction unnecessary. But he was skeptical that Trump would fulfill the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement's sweeping ambitions. ''It would be great if we didn't have to do so much, but my bet is that we will,'' he said. So in the coming years, he expects \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists to launch ''more direct action, putting our bodies on the line between the victim and the oppressor, regardless of what the consequences might be.''\par \par \uc2 In his first days in office, Trump hasn't made curtailing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a major priority. His Justice Department has not, so far, acceded to requests from the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement to declare the mailing of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills illegal under the 19th-century Comstock Act, though that could easily change. At his confirmation hearing on Wednesday, \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2 , Trump's nominee to head the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2 , left the door open to restricting the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -inducing drug mifepristone, which the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement claims, falsely, is unsafe. But he didn't promise to move right away. ''President Trump has asked me to study the safety of mifepristone,'' said Kennedy. ''He has not yet taken a stand on how to regulate it.''\par \par \uc2 What the president has done, however, is create a newly indulgent environment for activists like Geraghty, who use their bodies to try to physically disrupt clinic operations. Shortly after pardoning the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  demonstrators, Trump's Justice Department announced that it plans to stop enforcing the Freedom of Access to Clinic Entrances, or FACE, Act, except in ''extraordinary circumstances'' or where there are ''significant aggravating factors.'' That law was passed in 1994 in response to a spate of violence ranging from mass \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic blockades -- participants called them ''rescues'' -- to an assassination of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider. Among other things, FACE made it a federal crime to use physical force to intimidate or obstruct those seeking to provide or obtain abortions, or to intentionally damage an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic. ''There's a message being sent that there is no legal risk anymore to doing the kinds of things that the FACE Act says are illegal,'' said Mary Ziegler, a legal scholar and the author of the forthcoming book ''Personhood: The New Civil War Over Reproduction.''\par \par \uc2 To hear Republicans talk about it, those convicted under the FACE Act were simply peaceful protesters prosecuted for their beliefs. Court documents suggest otherwise. In the 2020 protest Geraghty took part in, for example, a group of activists shoved their way into the waiting room of the Washington Surgi-Clinic. A nurse sprained her ankle while struggling unsuccessfully to hold the door shut against one of them. Once inside, some of the activists chained themselves together, barricading the doors to the treatment area, while others blocked the entrances. One patient, who'd traveled from Ohio to end her pregnancy after finding out that her fetus had a grave and almost certainly fatal abnormality, had already taken a labor-inducing drug that's sometimes used in later abortions. She and her husband pleaded with the activists to let them in. Then she collapsed in pain in front of them.\par \par \uc2 The president's actions seem likely to encourage more such clinic invasions. This nod-and-wink approach to anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  vigilantism is quintessentially Trumpian, allowing him to cater to his extremist followers while maintaining a veneer of plausible deniability. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  divides Trump's supporters, which is why he pivoted from his most hard-core anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions during the last presidential campaign. (''My Administration will be great for women and their \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' he wrote on Truth Social in August.) Were he to suddenly end access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, some of his pro-choice voters might be upset. That doesn't mean he won't eventually do it, but it explains why, even as he moves quickly on many other parts of the right's agenda, he seems to be punting a decision on medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the future.\par \par \uc2 While the FACE Act is important to activists, there's little public awareness of its importance. By undermining it, Trump can reward his base without risking broader backlash. There is something particularly combustible, however, about a situation in which the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement is frustrated on a policy level but given tacit permission to break laws.\par \par \uc2 The FACE Act wasn't the only reason that the rescue movement of the late 1980s and early 1990s fell apart, but it played a role. A lot of people involved ''dropped out when the FACE Act was hanging over them, because the risks were just too high,'' said Ziegler. Before going to prison, Geraghty had been part of a group of people trying to revive such tactics. On Monday, he said he expected the movement to grow. ''I can't answer the question of why rescue is back,'' he said, ''but it's back, baby.''\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Instagram\uc2  , TikTok , WhatsApp , X and Threads .\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250202el220006d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
