\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 N.Y. Doctor Indicted Over Sending  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pill\b0\par\par\uc2 By Pam Belluck and Emily Cochrane \par \uc2 1,392 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 February 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The case opens a new front in the battle between states that ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and states that support providing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  anywhere in the country.\par \par \uc2 A state grand jury in Louisiana has indicted a New York doctor for providing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to a Louisiana resident. The case appears to be the first time criminal charges have been filed against an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider for sending pills into a state with an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 The charges mark a new chapter in an escalating showdown between states that ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and those that want to protect and expand access to it. It is challenging one of the foremost strategies used by states that support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights: shield laws intended to provide legal protection to doctors who prescribe and send \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to states with bans.\par \par \uc2 The charges were brought against Dr. Margaret Carpenter, who was operating under New York's telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  shield law, which stipulates that New York authorities will not cooperate with prosecutions or other legal actions filed against New York \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers by other states.\par \par \uc2 Telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  shield laws, which have been adopted by eight states so far, have become a significant avenue for providing access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for women in states with bans without requiring them to leave their state. Doctors, nurse practitioners and other health care providers in states with shield laws have been sending more than 10,000 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills per month to states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans or restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Legal experts said the case ratchets up the legal wars over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and will almost certainly end up in federal court and possibly the Supreme Court. It is expected to become a major test of whether states can apply criminal laws to people acting outside their borders.\par \par \uc2 Since the Supreme Court's 2022 decision in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization overturning the national right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the United States has been divided between states that restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and states that protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''There's just been a sense that if you were in a blue state, you're shielded from the consequences of Dobbs,'' said Mary Ziegler, a law professor and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  expert at the \uc2 University of California, Davis\uc2 . ''Prosecutions like this undermine that assumption, and we don't know exactly how, or how much, but you can't take that for granted.''\par \par \uc2 Federal courts will have to sort out ''where the line will be drawn and even which precedents the courts will be willing to overrule,'' she said. ''It's not clear what will happen.''\par \par \uc2 The Louisiana indictment, by a grand jury in West Baton Rouge Parish, follows what is believed to be the first civil suit filed against an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider in a shield-law state. That case was filed in December by the Texas attorney general, \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , also against Dr. Carpenter, for prescribing and sending pills to a woman in Texas.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, Tony Clayton, the district attorney who oversees West Baton Rouge, said in an interview, ''I just don't know under what theory could a doctor be thinking that you should ship your pills to Louisiana to abort our citizens' babies.''\par \par \uc2 He added: ''The pill may be legal in New York. It's not legal in Louisiana.''\par \par \uc2 In response to the charges, Gov. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  of New York said in a video posted on X, ''I will never, under any circumstances, turn this doctor over to the state of Louisiana under any extradition request.'' She pledged ''to do everything I can to protect this doctor and allow her to continue the work that she's doing that is so essential.''\par \par \uc2 The use of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication has grown significantly in recent years. Medication abortions now account for nearly two-thirds of pregnancy terminations in the United States. The method is typically used through 12 weeks of pregnancy and involves two drugs -- mifepristone, which stops a pregnancy from developing, followed a day or two later by misoprostol, which causes contractions similar to a miscarriage.\par \par \uc2 In 2021, the Food and Drug Administration lifted a rule requiring patients to obtain mifepristone in person, allowing the medication to be sent through the mail.\par \par \uc2 The ability to mail the medications, bolstered by shield laws, has made it much more difficult for states with bans to prevent their residents from getting access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The actions filed against Dr. Carpenter in Texas and Louisiana are part of a campaign to limit that access.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents are also pressing the Trump administration to revive a 151-year-old federal anti-vice law known as the Comstock Act and use it to try to prevent the mailing of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills.\par \par \uc2 In the Louisiana case, the grand jury indicted Dr. Carpenter and her medical practice for ''criminal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  by means of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -inducing drugs.''\par \par \uc2 Dr. Carpenter, of New Paltz, N.Y., did not comment on the case on Friday, and efforts to reach lawyers representing her were unsuccessful.\par \par \uc2 The court documents, which include few details, indicate that the case involved a girl who was under 18 whose mother ordered \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills and gave them to her in April 2024. The mother was also charged with violating the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Clayton, the West Baton Rouge district attorney, said the authorities became aware of the case after a police officer responded to a 911 call placed by the teenager.\par \par \uc2 ''The officer at the time thought he was dealing with a child who was having a miscarriage,'' Mr. Clayton said. After the police took the teenager to a hospital, the authorities learned that she had taken \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication and the investigation became criminal, he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Clayton, who declined to disclose the age or other details about the girl, said that ''the evidence will show that the child had planned a reveal party'' and did not want an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He said that charges would not be filed against the girl.\par \par \uc2 Police records show that the mother, whose name \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  is not disclosing to protect the identity of her daughter, was arrested and released on bond. Attempts to reach her on Friday were unsuccessful.\par \par \uc2 ''The allegations in this case have nothing to do with \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care,'' said Liz Murrill, the state attorney general. ''This is about coercion. This is about forcing somebody to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  who didn't want one.''\par \par \uc2 The attorney general of New York, Letitia James, said in a statement, ''This cowardly attempt out of Louisiana to weaponize the law against out-of-state providers is unjust and un-American.''\par \par \uc2 She added: ''Medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is safe, effective and necessary, and New York will ensure that it remains available to all Americans who need it.''\par \par \uc2 Dr. Carpenter is a specialist in \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a co-founder of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Coalition for Telemedicine, an organization that advocates access to telehealth \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in all 50 states.\par \par \uc2 ''Shield laws across the country enable licensed health care professionals to successfully deliver \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care to patients in under-resourced areas nationwide,'' the coalition said in a statement on Friday, adding, ''This state-sponsored effort to prosecute a doctor providing safe and effective care should alarm everyone.''\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists praised the Louisiana charges.\par \par \uc2 ''This case exposes how mail-order \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drugs are fueling an epidemic of coercion, a new form of domestic violence against mothers and their babies,'' Katie Daniel, director of legal affairs for SBA Pro-Life America, said in a statement. The statement commended Louisiana for tightening laws against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication and said, ''In blue states, pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politicians are doing the polar opposite, shielding abortionists.''\par \par \uc2 In the Texas lawsuit, Dr. Carpenter was accused of providing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to a 20-year-old woman in July. The suit said the woman later asked the ''biological father of her unborn child'' to take her to the emergency room because of ''severe bleeding,'' and he learned at that time that she was nine weeks pregnant.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Paxton said that by filing the Texas lawsuit, he was seeking to have the court stop Dr. Carpenter from continuing to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication to patients in Texas, and to apply Texas' ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to her. The ban carries a penalty of at least $100,000 for each violation.\par \par \uc2 Kirsten Noyes contributed research.\par \par \uc2 Kirsten Noyes contributed research.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250201el2100041\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
