\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Opponents of  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Raise Their Ambitions\b0\par\par\uc2 By Elizabeth Dias and Lisa Lerer \par \uc2 1,161 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 January 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 19\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The March for Life on Friday brought together a movement invigorated by some early moves of the second Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists are charging ahead with their ultimate mission to end all abortions nationwide, freshly emboldened by powerful allies in Washington, a continued Supreme Court majority and legislative opportunities in conservative states.\par \par \uc2 At the March for Life on Friday, the three most powerful men in America gave remarks: President Trump via recorded video, and Vice President \uc2 JD Vance\uc2  and Speaker Mike Johnson in person. On Thursday Mr. Trump pardoned 23 activists convicted of obstructing access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics.\par \par \uc2 And buried in an executive order stating that the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  would recognize only two sexes -- male and female -- was a phrase that caught the attention of activists hoping to give constitutional rights to embryos. People were male or female ''at conception,'' the order stated.\par \par \uc2 ''This administration stands by you, we stand with you, and most importantly we stand with the most vulnerable,'' Vice President J.D. Vance told the crowd of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protesters gathered on the National Mall on Friday. ''America is fundamentally a pro-baby, a pro-family and a pro-life country.''\par \par \uc2 Positioning himself as the leader of the next generation of their movement, Mr. Vance promised to protect Christians and anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protesters from federal prosecution, saying he would attend the annual protest every year.\par \par \uc2 Standing among the thousands of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protesters -- largely school and church groups and families cheering in the cold as speakers praised Mr. Trump's anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  actions -- were three members of the president's domestic policy council.\par \par \uc2 Such steps and symbolism amounted to a signal after a presidential campaign during which Mr. Trump pushed anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaders to the outskirts of the Republican Party. After the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade two years ago with the Dobbs decision, many Republicans worried that the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cause had become a political liability among independent voters.\par \par \uc2 But now that electoral politicking is over, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists are facing the question of just how far they can go.\par \par \uc2 Whether the recent signals indicate a more aggressive approach remains to be seen. Few believe that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a top priority for the president in the same way as immigration or trade. How far he will move to limit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide -- or if he will take any steps at all -- is unclear. But he's also unlikely, allies and activists say, to try to impede conservative states' efforts to enact new restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2 , Republican of Missouri, spoke with Mr. Trump on Thursday and expressed hope that the president would restore policies under his first administration, like the requirement that women pick up \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication in person. Such a measure would hamper the ability of women in states where the procedure is banned to receive the pills through the mail.\par \par \uc2 ''Leadership from the federal level is important,'' Mr. Hawley said. ''But in terms of the changes in the law, after Dobbs, the states are in many ways the most important theaters.''\par \par \uc2 When asked about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the campaign, Mr. Trump said that the federal government ''should have nothing to do with this issue.'' But in appearances before Christian organizations, Mr. Trump pledged his staunch support, promising to stand with them ''side by side.''\par \par \uc2 Many anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists were disappointed when he selected \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2 , who has supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, to lead the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  -- a primary agency where they hoped to advance their cause.\par \par \uc2 In private meetings with senators like Mr. Hawley who oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, Mr. Kennedy pledged to uphold the president's agenda. But that did little to reassure some activists.\par \par \uc2 ''The answer in Senate meetings and that we get back over and over is that Kennedy's position will be what the Trump position is,'' Marjorie Dannenfelser, president of Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America, said. ''What we are looking forward to is knowing exactly what that means.''\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists are warning of far more sweeping actions. They believe that the administration will prosecute \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and enforce the Comstock Act, a dormant law from 1873, to criminalize the shipping of any materials used in an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, which account for the majority of abortions in America.\par \par \uc2 ''Let's go back to 2016, 2017, when people said, 'Oh, yeah, yeah, he talked about Roe, but he's not going to do that,''' said Elisabeth Smith, the state policy director for the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ''We're in that same place where people understandably cannot accept what is coming because it is too awful to accept, and it is against the will of the people.''\par \par \uc2 Regardless of any federal actions, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists are proposing a deluge of legislation in states, including ways to weaken \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections passed by voters, and to raise penalties on women and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in states where the procedure is banned. The moves are designed both to restrict access and to generate new fights in the courts, where they believe they have the upper hand.\par \par \uc2 Conservative lawmakers in states including South Carolina and Oklahoma are trying to advance legislation that would allow women who have abortions to be prosecuted for homicide. In Ohio, where voters codified \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights into the State Constitution in 2023, the Republican attorney general is arguing in court that longstanding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions -- including a 24-hour waiting period before receiving the procedure -- are still legal.\par \par \uc2 Some activists are looking to Texas, long an incubator for new restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and where nearly all abortions are now banned, as a model for next steps.\par \par \uc2 John Seago, the president of Texas Right to Life, said that stopping the flow of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication into his state was a top priority. Republicans have filed a series of bills targeting access to the pills, including classifying \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medications as dangerous controlled substances and online \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication sales as a deceptive trade practice. Republicans have also introduced legislation allowing citizens to sue internet providers for hosting websites that sell \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication -- a proposal that relies on the same novel enforcement mechanism included in the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 ''The federal government can definitely help on a lot of these issues, if they are willing to, but we're not holding our breath for our friends in D.C. to really lead on this,'' Mr. Seago said.\par \par \uc2 At the March for Life on Friday, at least, the Trump administration figured prominently. Jennie Bradley Lichter, the incoming president of the March for Life, was deputy director of the Domestic Policy Council during Mr. Trump's first term. She pointed to the high-level speakers on Friday as an optimistic sign.\par \par \uc2 ''I just look back at the president's record from the first time around, and I anticipate that we will see some great wins,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250126el1q0004k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
