\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Meta\uc2  Blocked Posts Selling  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pills\b0\par\par\uc2 By Claire Cain Miller, Kate Conger and Mike Isaac \par \uc2 690 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 January 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Some posts related to obtaining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills were recently hidden on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and some accounts were suspended, before being later restored.\par \par \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and \uc2 Facebook\uc2  have recently blurred, blocked or removed posts from two \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill providers. \uc2 Instagram\uc2  also suspended the accounts of several \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill providers and hid the providers from appearing in search and recommendations.\par \par \uc2 The actions ramped up in the last two weeks, and were especially noticeable in the last two days, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill providers said. Content from their accounts -- or in some cases, their entire accounts -- were no longer visible on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2 , which owns \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , confirmed some account suspensions and the blurring of posts. The company restored some of the accounts and posts on Thursday, after \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  asked about the actions.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  has been under scrutiny since \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2 , its chief executive, announced sweeping changes to the company's speech policies earlier this month. Mr. Zuckerberg vowed to loosen restrictions on online speech, causing concerns among misinformation researchers and others that the shifts could cause a spike in hate speech and have other harmful effects.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  said the moderation of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -focused accounts was not related to the change in speech policies. But the timing of the incidents raised questions about whether the company was really loosening speech restrictions, and was another example of its challenges in content enforcement.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Meta\uc2  spokesman attributed some of the recent incidents involving \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill-related posts and accounts to rules that prohibit the sale of pharmaceutical drugs on its platforms without proper certification. The company also described some of the incidents as ''over-enforcement.''\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2 , which has previously suppressed posts from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, has said that it was making changes to its speech policies partly to reduce the number of posts that were erroneously taken down.\par \par \uc2 ''We've been quite clear in recent weeks that we want to allow more speech and reduce enforcement mistakes,'' \uc2 Meta\uc2  said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Lisa Femia, a staff attorney at the \uc2 Electronic Frontier Foundation\uc2 , said that since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade in 2022, ''there's been a massive uptick in social media platforms removing content related to \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care and specifically \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills. This is an ongoing, increasing problem and a real threat to people receiving vital information and guidance about health care online.''\par \par \uc2 Aid Access, one of the largest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill providers in the United States, said some posts were removed on its \uc2 Facebook\uc2  account and blurred out on its \uc2 Instagram\uc2  account since November, with more posts blurred in recent days. The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill service said it has been blocked from accessing its \uc2 Facebook\uc2  account since November, and its \uc2 Instagram\uc2  account was suspended last week, though it has since been restored.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Instagram\uc2  accounts of other \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill providers, including Women Help Women and Just the Pill, were also suspended in recent days. The providers said the reason that \uc2 Meta\uc2  gave them for the suspensions was that their accounts did not ''follow our Community Standards on guns, drugs and other restricted goods.'' Both accounts were restored on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Instagram\uc2  account of Hey Jane, another \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill provider, was recently invisible in \uc2 Instagram\uc2  search, said Rebecca Davis, who leads marketing at Hey Jane. Something similar happened in 2023 until \uc2 Meta\uc2  reversed it, she said.\par \par \uc2 ''We know firsthand that this suppression actively prevents Hey Jane from reaching people who are seeking out timely health care information,'' Ms. Davis said. ''Given \uc2 Meta\uc2 's recent promises around free speech, we're incredibly disappointed to see how the platform is restricting our free speech.''\par \par \uc2 The Food and Drug Administration\uc2  permits telehealth providers to prescribe online and deliver by mail the prescription drugs that cause an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , mifepristone and misoprostol. Twelve states have banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and more have placed gestational limits or restrictions on mail-order pills. But providers in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal have been mailing pills to states with bans under shield laws meant to protect them.\par \par \uc2 Sheera Frenkel contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Sheera Frenkel contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250125el1p0003e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
