\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Study Citing Alternative to  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pill May Tangle Morning-After Pill in Politics\b0\par\par\uc2 By Pam Belluck and Emily Bazelon \par \uc2 1,607 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 January 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 13\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The research could further complicate the polarized politics of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  because the drug in the study is the key ingredient in a pill used for emergency contraception.\par \par \uc2 A new study suggests a possible alternative to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill mifepristone, a drug that continues to be a target of lawsuits and legislation from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents.\par \par \uc2 But the potential substitute could further complicate the politics of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  because it is also the key ingredient in a contraceptive morning-after pill.\par \par \uc2 The new study, published Thursday in the journal NEJM Evidence, involved a drug called ulipristal acetate, the active ingredient in the prescription contraceptive Ella, one of two types of morning-after pills approved in the United States. (The other, Plan B One-Step, which does not require a prescription, contains a different drug and does not work in a way that would terminate a pregnancy, according to scientific evidence.)\par \par \uc2 In the study, 133 women who were up to nine weeks' pregnant took twice the dose of the ulipristal acetate contained in Ella, followed by misoprostol, the second drug used in the typical medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimen. All but four of the women completed the termination of their pregnancies without further intervention, a 97 percent completion rate that is similar to the regimen using mifepristone. (The others finished the process with additional medication or a procedure.)\par \par \uc2 There were no serious complications, and the study concluded that using ulipristal acetate in the two-drug medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimen was safe.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Beverly Winikoff, the lead author of the study and the president of Gynuity Health Projects, a \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  research organization, said that after the Supreme Court overturned the national right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2022, she became increasingly interested in a possible role for ulipristal acetate, which has a similar chemical structure to mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 ''I was thinking, there's maybe something else we can do,'' she said. ''Another option. And this one is already on the market.''\par \par \uc2 The political implications of the study are complex because of their potential to blur the line between the public perception of emergency contraception and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . For years, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents have opposed morning-after pills by saying they can cause abortions, and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  experts have countered by pointing to scientific evidence that the pills do not terminate pregnancies but instead act to prevent pregnancy after sex.\par \par \uc2 Some \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  experts are concerned that research showing that a morning-after pill ingredient can be used for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could stoke attempts to crack down on emergency contraception and sow confusion that could bolster the larger anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy.\par \par \uc2 Kristi Hamrick, a spokeswoman for Students for Life of America, said her organization would ''absolutely'' consider litigation over Ella.\par \par \uc2 ''The pro-life movement should be vindicated,'' Ms. Hamrick said. ''We've been arguing for years that Ella acts as an abortifacient.''\par \par \uc2 Mary Ziegler, a law professor and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  expert at the \uc2 University of California, Davis\uc2 , said the study could present challenges for both sides of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate.\par \par \uc2 ''It's going to put wind in the sails of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents who have been saying things like contraceptives can be abortifacients,'' she said, adding, ''This study being released will be difficult, I think, for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters to manage.''\par \par \uc2 But Ms. Ziegler said the study's findings could also be ''politically risky'' for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents because public support for contraception is high and many voters in conservative states endorsed ballot measures protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. ''I think it's one of those things that's going to tempt social conservatives to push probably faster than politics would currently permit in the direction of regulating contraception,'' she said. ''And I think that could backfire.''\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  experts said the new study did not refute the science showing that morning-after pills don't induce abortions, because it involved a different dose of the drug.\par \par \uc2 Several experts said that because it was a relatively small first study with no comparison group of patients, more research was needed before ulipristal acetate should be used as a substitute for mifepristone in the two-drug regimen. ''We can't change clinical practice based on this study,'' said Kelly Cleland, a researcher who is the executive director of the American Society for Emergency Contraception.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents said they were not surprised by the study's findings.\par \par \uc2 ''After years of denying ulipristal acetate's potential to end the life of an embryo, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates are now starting to use it as a substitute for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drug mifepristone,'' Dr. Donna Harrison, director of research for the American Association of Pro-Life Obstetricians and Gynecologists, said in a statement. ''The reason for this is simple. Ulipristal and mifepristone function in the same way.''\par \par \uc2 Ella was already a target for some conservatives. Project 2025, a right-wing policy blueprint that has been strongly tied to the new Trump administration, said that Ella should be removed from required insurance coverage of contraception under the Affordable Care Act because it is ''a potential abortifacient.''\par \par \uc2 Mifepristone, the first pill in the standard two-drug medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimen, is the only drug specifically approved for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the United States. Typically used through 12 weeks' gestation, mifepristone stops the development of a pregnancy by blocking the hormone progesterone. The second drug, misoprostol, is taken 24 to 48 hours later and causes contractions similar to a miscarriage.\par \par \uc2 Ulipristal acetate is in the same class of medications as mifepristone and also blocks the activity of progesterone, a hormone that prepares the uterus to receive and hold an embryo, said Dr. \uc2 Daniel Grossman\uc2 , a \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  physician and researcher at the \uc2 University of California, San Francisco\uc2 , who was not involved in the new study.\par \par \uc2 In the study, a 60 milligram dose of ulipristal acetate (double the 30 milligrams in Ella) was substituted for mifepristone and followed by misoprostol, which has various medical uses and has not been targeted as much by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents. (Misoprostol can also facilitate an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its own, but is considered more effective in a combination regimen.)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  experts said they welcomed the search for alternatives to mifepristone because \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents have been waging efforts to sharply restrict the medication across the country, most notably with a federal lawsuit against the Food and Drug Administration. The Supreme Court rejected that lawsuit last year, ruling that the initial plaintiffs lacked standing to sue, but the suit has since been revived with three states as plaintiffs.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Grossman, who wrote an editorial about the study, said the prospect of a substitute for mifepristone was ''certainly a promising finding.'' But, he added, ''if because of this new evidence that at higher doses, ulipristal acetate could cause an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that were to lead to ulipristal acetate being taken off the market for emergency contraception, that would be really, really bad.''\par \par \uc2 Perrigo, the company that manufactures Ella, issued a statement saying that because the new study tested ulipristal acetate at a higher dose than one pill of Ella, and in combination with misoprostol, ''there continues to be no evidence to show that, on its own, Ella causes an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .'' The company added that ''Ella is an F.D.A.-approved emergency contraception pill that acts before pregnancy can occur.''\par \par \uc2 The study was conducted in Mexico City and was co-led by researchers there.\par \par \uc2 Scientists have long understood that hormone-based drugs may be able to play different functions at different doses along the spectrum of a woman's \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  cycle.\par \par \uc2 In Europe, researchers including Dr. Rebecca Gomperts, a Dutch physician and founder of telemedicine organizations that provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills globally, are studying low doses of mifepristone as a weekly birth control pill. Dr. Gomperts said she considered the new ulipristal acetate study sufficient to prescribe the drug off-label in combination with misoprostol.\par \par \uc2 ''The more uses we have for these medications, the harder it will be for people to take them away,'' said Dr. Paul Blumenthal, an emeritus professor of obstetrics and gynecology at \uc2 Stanford University\uc2  who was in an advisory group for the study.\par \par \uc2 Plan B is a much more widely used morning-after pill in the United States, but Ella is considered more effective for some women, including people who are overweight. Plan B is intended to be taken within three days after unprotected sex, while Ella can be taken within five days.\par \par \uc2 Both pills prevent pregnancy by blocking ovulation, the release of eggs from the ovaries that occurs before eggs can be fertilized, scientific studies have shown.\par \par \uc2 The claim by some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents that morning-after pills are \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drugs is based on a theory that they might also prevent a fertilized egg from implanting in the womb. Most scientific research has not found that to be the case.\par \par \uc2 For years, despite scientific evidence to the contrary detailed in an investigation by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , the F.D.A.-approved label and packaging for Plan B One-Step said that while the pill worked by blocking ovulation, there was a possibility it might prevent implantation. In 2022, the agency changed the language to make it clear that Plan B acts only before fertilization, ''will not work if you're already pregnant, and will not affect an existing pregnancy.''\par \par \uc2 The F.D.A. label for Ella says that its ''likely primary mechanism of action'' is to stop or delay ovulation. The label adds that the medication may also affect implantation. Studies in recent years, however, suggest that Ella does not operate by blocking a fertilized egg from implanting in the womb.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250125el1p00037\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 rst=nytf AND (abortion OR "reproductive rights" OR "reproductive health") AND (atleast5(abortion) OR atleast3("reproductive rights"))\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Date\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 01/01/2021 to 06/30/2026\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\b